/* Simulated instructor-grounded differential cases, not actual patient records. */
window.VPQ_NECROPSY_DIFFERENTIAL_CASES={"version":8,"source":"Simulated pathology differential cases derived from instructor slide-audited Lectures 68, 69, 70, 72, 73, 74, 75, 77, 78 and 79; no actual patient records or slide images are reproduced.","cases":[{"id":"n68-brainstem","lecture":"68","system":"Neuro","title":"Circling ruminant: where is the lesion?","species":"Adult sheep","history":"An adult sheep fed poor-quality silage develops circling, head tilt, drooling and unilateral facial weakness. At necropsy, the brain has little obvious gross change; faint discoloration may be present in the brainstem.","gross":"Brainstem-focused lesion with little or no obvious gross change.","histo":"Multifocal neutrophilic microabscesses in brainstem parenchyma, sometimes mixed inflammatory cuffs around vessels.","best":"Listeriosis (Listeria monocytogenes)","morph":"Brainstem: multifocal, suppurative microabscessing encephalitis","mechanism":"Listeria can ascend via trigeminal and other cranial nerves by retrograde axonal transport, explaining the brainstem focus and asymmetric cranial nerve deficits.","tests":"Culture/PCR or other appropriate pathogen detection on relevant fresh tissues; necropsy localization and histology support but do not alone prove the agent.","ref":"Lecture 68 — Listeriosis, slide 10","options":[{"name":"Listeriosis (Listeria monocytogenes)","supports":"Silage exposure, circling, cranial nerve signs and brainstem localization all support this cause.","against":"Cannot confirm Listeria from these clinical signs alone.","hist":"Microabscessing suppurative rhombencephalitis.","role":"best"},{"name":"Thrombotic meningoencephalitis (Histophilus somni)","supports":"Ruminants may develop septic neurologic disease.","against":"Usually young feedlot cattle with random hemorrhagic foci and thrombosis/vasculitis rather than neural ascent to brainstem.","hist":"Septic vasculitis, thrombi and hemorrhagic infarction.","role":"less"},{"name":"Rabies (Lyssavirus)","supports":"Can cause cranial nerve signs and nonsuppurative brainstem inflammation.","against":"Typical rabies microscopy is nonsuppurative polioencephalomyelitis, not neutrophilic microabscesses.","hist":"Lymphocytic inflammation and possible Negri bodies (not invariably present).","role":"less"},{"name":"Equine protozoal myeloencephalitis","supports":"Asymmetric neurologic signs can occur with some CNS parasitic/protozoal conditions.","against":"Predominantly equine and frequently hemorrhagic/necrotizing spinal cord lesions; host argues strongly against.","hist":"Necrosis, hemorrhage, mixed inflammation and protozoal organisms occasionally detectable.","role":"unlikely"}]},{"id":"n68-feedlot","lecture":"68","system":"Neuro","title":"Feedlot calf with hemorrhagic brain lesions","species":"8-month-old feedlot steer","history":"The steer develops fever and acute neurologic signs. Multiple irregular, dark-red foci are scattered at the cerebral gray–white interface.","gross":"Multifocal irregular cerebral hemorrhage and necrosis, sometimes with swelling or cloudy CSF.","histo":"Numerous small vessels show fibrinoid/necrotizing vasculitis, thrombi and adjacent infarction with hemorrhage.","best":"Thrombotic meningoencephalitis (Histophilus somni)","morph":"Brain: multifocal, hemorrhagic, necrotizing, thrombotic meningoencephalitis","mechanism":"H. somni bacteremia damages endothelium, initiating thrombosis, ischemic necrosis and leakage of blood. Distribution is vascular rather than perineural.","tests":"Bacterial testing of appropriate fresh CNS/systemic samples plus histopathology; consider systemic pneumonia, myocarditis or polyarthritis.","ref":"Lecture 68 — TME slides 11–12","options":[{"name":"Thrombotic meningoencephalitis (Histophilus somni)","supports":"Feedlot age, multifocal hemorrhage, gray–white junction lesions and thrombi/vasculitis are compelling.","against":"Agent still needs confirmatory identification.","hist":"Vasculitis, thrombosis, infarction.","role":"best"},{"name":"Listeriosis","supports":"Ruminant neurologic signs.","against":"Brainstem microabscesses and circling are more typical than multifocal cerebral vascular hemorrhage.","hist":"Neutrophilic brainstem microabscesses.","role":"less"},{"name":"Clostridium perfringens type D enterotoxemia","supports":"Can injure CNS vascular barrier and cause malacia.","against":"Often sheep/goats after ration change, preferentially bilaterally symmetric white-matter damage, rather than irregular thrombotic hemorrhages.","hist":"Edema and symmetric necrotic white matter foci.","role":"less"},{"name":"EHV-1 myeloencephalopathy","supports":"Vasculitis with thrombosis and hemorrhage can occur.","against":"Typically horses, not feedlot cattle; epidemiology argues strongly against it.","hist":"Small-vessel vasculitis, thrombosis and necrosis.","role":"unlikely"}]},{"id":"n69-white-matter","lecture":"69","system":"Neuro","title":"Horse with acute cerebral malacia","species":"Adult horse","history":"A horse that has eaten contaminated moldy corn develops abrupt blindness, head pressing and seizures. On sectioning the brain, cerebral white matter is softened and liquefied while much of the gray matter is relatively spared.","gross":"Pale/yellow softened or cavitated regions concentrated in cerebral white matter.","histo":"Liquefactive necrosis and degeneration of white matter, with macrophages during lesion evolution.","best":"Fumonisin B1 equine leukoencephalomalacia","morph":"Cerebrum: multifocal to regionally extensive leukoencephalomalacia","mechanism":"Fusarium-associated fumonisin B1 toxicosis causes preferential severe injury to equine cerebral white matter. The professor notes its tissue selectivity is incompletely explained.","tests":"Feed/exposure history and toxin testing support the association; evaluate the lesion distribution and exclude other toxic/vascular causes.","ref":"Lecture 69 — Fumonisin B1 slide 35","options":[{"name":"Fumonisin B1 equine leukoencephalomalacia","supports":"Horse, moldy feed and white-matter liquefaction are the strongest combination.","against":"Specific fungal toxin exposure requires supporting feed/history evidence.","hist":"Severe white matter necrosis.","role":"best"},{"name":"Thiamine-deficiency polioencephalomalacia","supports":"Neurologic blindness, seizures and malacia.","against":"Classic ruminant lesion primarily affects cerebral cortical GRAY matter; species and location diverge.","hist":"Laminar cortical necrosis and softening.","role":"less"},{"name":"Centaurea-associated nigropallidal encephalomalacia","supports":"Horse toxic plant exposure and malacia possible.","against":"Preferential bilateral substantia nigra/globus pallidus lesions with chewing/prehension problems, not diffuse cerebral white matter injury.","hist":"Targeted neuronal necrosis in basal nuclei.","role":"less"},{"name":"Fibrocartilaginous embolism","supports":"Abrupt ischemic CNS malacia.","against":"Commonly spinal cord rather than this dietary-linked equine cerebral white matter syndrome.","hist":"Fibrocartilage occluding spinal vessel.","role":"unlikely"}]},{"id":"n70-flaccid","lecture":"70","system":"PNS","title":"Acute flaccid paralysis with no gross nerve lesion","species":"Horse","history":"A horse develops progressively weak tongue, eyelids and pharyngeal muscles, then generalized flaccid paralysis after possible access to decaying carcass material. Gross sections of brain, cord and nerves appear unremarkable.","gross":"No characteristic gross CNS or PNS lesion despite severe neuromuscular dysfunction.","histo":"No specific microscopic lesion required; neurotoxin affects synaptic signaling rather than visibly destroying neurons.","best":"Botulism (Clostridium botulinum toxin)","morph":"Functional presynaptic neuromuscular blockade (often no characteristic morphologic lesion)","mechanism":"Botulinum neurotoxin blocks presynaptic acetylcholine release at neuromuscular junctions, causing flaccid paralysis.","tests":"Assess exposure history and use appropriate laboratory toxin testing in consultation with diagnostic laboratory; consider clinical differentials.","ref":"Lecture 70 — Botulism slides 18–19","options":[{"name":"Botulism (Clostridium botulinum toxin)","supports":"Flaccid generalized paralysis with cranial muscle involvement, carcass exposure and no structural lesion.","against":"Clinical presentation is not pathognomonic; toxin confirmation may be difficult.","hist":"No characteristic gross or microscopic lesion.","role":"best"},{"name":"Tetanus","supports":"Another clostridial neurologic toxicosis often without a gross lesion.","against":"Typical tetanus is SPASTIC rigidity due to loss of inhibitory motor control, not flaccid paralysis.","hist":"Often no characteristic neural lesion.","role":"less"},{"name":"Equine recurrent laryngeal neuropathy","supports":"Laryngeal dysfunction can contribute to abnormal airway signs.","against":"Chronic focal left laryngeal abductor muscle atrophy, not rapidly generalized paralysis of multiple muscle groups.","hist":"Distal axonal degeneration and denervation atrophy.","role":"less"},{"name":"Equine grass sickness","supports":"Autonomic/enteric dysfunction with minimal gross nerve lesions.","against":"Typically profound ileus, weight loss and dysautonomia rather than predominant neuromuscular junction paralysis.","hist":"Degeneration of autonomic ganglion neurons.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s72-crust","lecture":"72","system":"Skin","title":"Crusted muzzle lesions in a lamb","species":"Young lamb","history":"Several lambs develop proliferative crusts around their lips and muzzle after skin abrasions. A person handling them develops an ulcerated finger lesion. There is little respiratory illness in the affected lambs.","gross":"Thick raised proliferative crusting at mucocutaneous muzzle/lip sites.","histo":"Epidermal proliferation, ballooning degeneration and poxviral cytoplasmic inclusion bodies can occur.","best":"Contagious ecthyma (orf parapoxvirus)","morph":"Muzzle/lips: multifocal proliferative and crusting viral dermatitis","mechanism":"Parapoxvirus enters damaged skin and replicates in proliferating epidermal keratinocytes; zoonotic transmission is possible.","tests":"Clinical herd distribution and viral PCR where needed; use gloves and protective handling because lesions can be zoonotic.","ref":"Lecture 72 — Orf slide 11; sheeppox slides 6–7","options":[{"name":"Contagious ecthyma (orf parapoxvirus)","supports":"Young sheep, lip/muzzle proliferative crusts, abrasions and zoonotic human lesion.","against":"Poxvirus identity still requires testing if outbreak differential is important.","hist":"Proliferative viral epidermal injury and possible cytoplasmic inclusions.","role":"best"},{"name":"Sheeppox (capripoxvirus)","supports":"Sheep and skin papules/pustules/crusts can overlap.","against":"More generalized severe systemic disease, sparsely woolled skin and possible visceral lesions in many presentations.","hist":"Poxviral epidermal damage and inflammation.","role":"less"},{"name":"Dermatophilosis (Dermatophilus congolensis)","supports":"Crusted exudative bacterial dermatitis can resemble pox crusts.","against":"Typical rain-associated fleece/dorsal disease and bacterial branching filaments; zoonotic transmission context and oral localization fit orf better.","hist":"Segmented Gram-positive branching 'railroad-track' organisms in crust.","role":"less"},{"name":"Fungal dermatophytosis","supports":"Scaling and crusted alopecic lesions may occur.","against":"Follicular circular alopecia and fungal hairs are more characteristic than proliferative mucocutaneous crusts.","hist":"Fungal hyphae/arthroconidia associated with hair/keratin.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s73-pruritus","lecture":"73","system":"Skin","title":"Itchy cats sharing a home","species":"Three domestic cats","history":"Two cats become intensely pruritic and overgroom, producing symmetrical ventral hair loss; a third cat has few signs. Surface tape samples may show small mites; deep follicular scrapings are less useful.","gross":"Self-induced symmetrical alopecia with variable subtle scaling and no obligate follicular pustules.","histo":"Superficial mites in stratum corneum if captured, rather than mites within hair follicles.","best":"Demodex gatoi infestation","morph":"Skin: superficial parasitic dermatitis / alopecia caused by grooming","mechanism":"D. gatoi is a contagious surface-dwelling feline Demodex species. Asymptomatic carriers can maintain transmission.","tests":"Superficial scrapings/tape and household contact assessment; confirm mite species and exclude allergic pruritus.","ref":"Lecture 73 — Feline Demodex cati versus gatoi slide 6","options":[{"name":"Demodex gatoi infestation","supports":"Multiple cats, contagious pruritus/overgrooming and surface-dwelling mites, with asymptomatic carrier.","against":"Mites may be difficult to find due to grooming; other pruritic dermatoses can coexist.","hist":"Mites in stratum corneum.","role":"best"},{"name":"Demodex cati infestation","supports":"Feline demodicosis may cause alopecia and sometimes pruritus.","against":"D. cati lives in follicles/sebaceous glands and is often associated with immunosuppression; does not fit surface mite clue.","hist":"Mites in hair follicles.","role":"less"},{"name":"Feline atopic dermatitis","supports":"Symmetric self-induced alopecia and pruritus fit.","against":"Contagion across cats and observed surface mites are not explained by atopy alone.","hist":"Variable hypersensitivity dermatitis without Demodex.","role":"less"},{"name":"Cheyletiellosis","supports":"Contagious superficial mites may affect multiple cats.","against":"Dorsal flaky 'walking dandruff' more characteristic than this ventral self-induced alopecia pattern.","hist":"Surface mites with scale.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s74-blister","lecture":"74","system":"Skin","title":"Pustules on muzzle and pawpads","species":"Middle-aged dog","history":"The dog develops symmetrical fragile pustules and thick crusts on the nasal planum, pinnae and pawpads. Several pustules have ruptured into collarettes and crusts.","gross":"Symmetrical superficial pustules and crusts at face and pawpads.","histo":"Subcorneal pustules with acantholytic keratinocytes and inflammatory cells; deep mucosal ulceration is absent.","best":"Pemphigus foliaceus","morph":"Skin: multifocal subcorneal acantholytic pustular dermatitis","mechanism":"Autoantibodies to keratinocyte desmosomal adhesion proteins, particularly desmocollin-1 emphasized in lecture, cause superficial loss of cohesion.","tests":"Biopsy a fresh intact pustule for histology; investigate infectious mimics such as staphylococcal pyoderma and dermatophytosis.","ref":"Lecture 74 — Pemphigus foliaceus slide 11, pemphigus vulgaris slide 12, bullous pemphigoid slides 14–15","options":[{"name":"Pemphigus foliaceus","supports":"Symmetric superficial pustules, face/pawpad distribution and subcorneal acantholysis fit.","against":"Acantholysis can also accompany some infections; correlate and exclude those.","hist":"Subcorneal acantholytic pustules.","role":"best"},{"name":"Pemphigus vulgaris","supports":"Immune-mediated acantholysis and erosions.","against":"Usually deeper suprabasal separation and severe mucosal/mucocutaneous ulcers.","hist":"Suprabasal acantholysis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Bullous pemphigoid","supports":"Autoimmune blistering with erosions and crusting.","against":"Subepidermal cleavage at basement membrane, not subcorneal acantholytic pustules.","hist":"Subepidermal blister.","role":"less"},{"name":"Superficial bacterial pyoderma","supports":"Pustules, collarettes and crusts can look similar grossly.","against":"Histology plus cytology identifying cocci and bacterial follicular infection favor pyoderma; symmetric footpad distribution fits PF better.","hist":"Bacteria-associated neutrophilic folliculitis/pustules.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r75-testis","lecture":"75","system":"Repro","title":"Retained testis and feminization in a dog","species":"9-year-old intact male dog","history":"The dog has an abdominal retained testis, symmetrical alopecia and gynecomastia. At surgery, the retained testis contains a large firm white lobulated mass. CBC shows anemia and thrombocytopenia.","gross":"Large firm white fibrotic intratesticular mass in retained abdominal testis.","histo":"Neoplastic sustentacular Sertoli cells form cords/tubules, often within collagenous stroma.","best":"Sertoli cell tumor","morph":"Testis: Sertoli-cell neoplasm with suspected estrogen-associated marrow suppression","mechanism":"Some Sertoli neoplasms secrete estrogens and produce feminization; high estrogen can suppress hematopoiesis causing anemia, leukopenia or thrombocytopenia.","tests":"Testicular histopathology; evaluate cytopenias and clinical endocrine findings. Gross alone cannot prove hormone secretion.","ref":"Lecture 75 — Sertoli tumor slides 24/26 and cryptorchidism slides 13–14","options":[{"name":"Sertoli cell tumor","supports":"Abdominal cryptorchidism, firm white mass, feminization and cytopenias strongly support functional Sertoli tumor.","against":"Hormonal effects occur in only some cases and require clinical correlation.","hist":"Sertoli cell cords/tubules in firm fibrous stroma.","role":"best"},{"name":"Seminoma","supports":"Older dog and cryptorchid testis predisposition.","against":"Usually soft gray-white bulging tumor and nonfunctional; feminization and fibrotic cut surface favor Sertoli.","hist":"Sheets of neoplastic germ cells.","role":"less"},{"name":"Leydig cell tumor","supports":"Testicular tumor can sometimes have hormonal activity.","against":"Usually small yellow soft greasy nodule rather than large firm white fibrous mass.","hist":"Nests of interstitial endocrine cells.","role":"less"},{"name":"Suppurative orchitis","supports":"Testicular enlargement may arise from inflammation.","against":"Would expect inflammatory exudate and neutrophils rather than discrete white neoplastic mass with feminizing syndrome.","hist":"Neutrophilic testicular inflammation.","role":"unlikely"}]},{"id":"r77-abortion","lecture":"77","system":"Repro","title":"Which placental lesion explains the abortion?","species":"Ewe with late-gestation abortion","history":"An ewe aborts in the last trimester. The fetus is well-preserved with few lesions. The cotyledons are dark red with dirty-red exudate; the intercotyledonary membranes are patchily thick, leathery and edematous. Another pregnant worker has helped handle abortion tissues.","gross":"Cotyledonary exudate AND extensive red-brown edematous/leathery intercotyledonary placentitis.","histo":"Necrotizing placentitis with inflammation/vasculitis can occur; intracellular organism identification requires ancillary tests.","best":"Chlamydia abortus (ovine enzootic abortion)","morph":"Placenta: multifocal to coalescing necrotizing exudative cotyledonary and intercotyledonary placentitis","mechanism":"C. abortus is an obligate intracellular zoonotic pathogen of sheep/goats; the instructor's speaker notes emphasize placental pathology in both compartments and late-pregnancy abortion.","tests":"Submit placenta and fetal samples for PCR/appropriate testing. Use zoonotic precautions, especially avoid exposure for pregnant people.","ref":"Lecture 77 Catch-Up PowerPoint speaker notes slides 40–41","options":[{"name":"Chlamydia abortus (ovine enzootic abortion)","supports":"Late ovine abortion, fetal preservation and BOTH placental compartments affected in characteristic leathery/exudative fashion.","against":"Other causes of severe placentitis overlap grossly; organism testing remains necessary.","hist":"Placental necrosis/vasculitis/inflammation; organisms can be difficult to distinguish without tests.","role":"best"},{"name":"Toxoplasma gondii","supports":"Ewe abortion and cotyledonary involvement.","against":"Classic distinctive scattered 1–3 mm white cotyledon nodules with largely normal intercotyledonary tissue, unlike broad leathery involvement.","hist":"Protozoal infection and focal placental necrosis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Coxiella burnetii (Q fever)","supports":"Late ovine abortion, thickened placenta, zoonotic risk and significant intercotyledonary exudate.","against":"Professor emphasizes abundant off-white exudate and often mineralization; can overlap heavily, so it remains a strong differential.","hist":"Marked placentitis with intracellular organisms; confirm by PCR.","role":"less"},{"name":"Campylobacter fetus subsp. fetus / C. jejuni","supports":"Sheep abortion and placentitis.","against":"More characteristic fetal targetoid liver necrosis when lesions are present; current description highlights placenta.","hist":"Fetal necrotizing hepatitis can occur.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"e78-corneal","lecture":"78","system":"Eye","title":"Dark corneal lesion in a cat","species":"Adult cat","history":"A cat has a history of recurrent corneal surface disease. A sharply defined brown-black plaque is present centrally on a cloudy cornea, with blood vessels extending toward the lesion.","gross":"Focal dark brown/black plaque surrounded by stromal opacity and corneal neovascularization.","histo":"Well-demarcated devitalized, acellular corneal stroma rather than intralesional hyphae or eosinophil-rich plaque.","best":"Feline corneal sequestrum","morph":"Cornea: focal stromal necrosis / sequestrum","mechanism":"Corneal sequestra represent focal stromal devitalization, often after chronic ulceration; precise initiating pathogenesis remains uncertain.","tests":"Ophthalmic exam and assessment for ulceration, depth and concurrent ocular disease; histopathology when tissue removed.","ref":"Lecture 78 — Corneal sequestrum slide 38; fungal and eosinophilic keratitis slides 34–35","options":[{"name":"Feline corneal sequestrum","supports":"Cat, focal dark devitalized plaque, surrounding opacity and neovascularization are characteristic.","against":"Concurrent corneal infection/ulceration may still require evaluation.","hist":"Acellular necrotic corneal stromal focus.","role":"best"},{"name":"Equine fungal keratitis","supports":"Corneal ulceration and severe necrosis can occur.","against":"More characteristic of horses; fungal hyphae and necrosuppurative keratitis versus focal black plaque in a cat.","hist":"Necrosuppurative keratitis with fungal hyphae.","role":"less"},{"name":"Feline eosinophilic keratitis","supports":"Cats may develop proliferative corneal plaques.","against":"Typically white-pink lateral corneal/conjunctival plaques, not a focal black necrotic sequestrum.","hist":"Predominantly eosinophilic infiltrates.","role":"less"},{"name":"Corneal dermoid","supports":"A congenital focal corneal lesion may resemble a mass.","against":"Dermoid contains hair follicles/adnexal glands rather than an acquired necrotic pigmented plaque.","hist":"Ectopic skin appendages in cornea.","role":"unlikely"}]},{"id":"ear79-bulla","lecture":"79","system":"Ear","title":"Older Cocker spaniel with a keratin-filled bulla","species":"Older Cocker Spaniel","history":"The dog has a long history of recurrent otitis externa/media and new head tilt. Necropsy reveals an expansile cyst-like lesion in the tympanic bulla packed with pale flaky lamellated material.","gross":"Expansile middle-ear cyst with keratinous contents, rather than free liquid pus.","histo":"Keratin within an epidermal-lined lesion with fibrovascular stroma, granulomatous inflammation and cholesterol clefts/crystals.","best":"Tympanokeratoma (aural cholesteatoma)","morph":"Middle ear: keratinizing epithelial cyst / tympanokeratoma","mechanism":"Epidermal cyst associated with tympanic membrane/chronic otitis expands with retained keratin, compressing/eroding surrounding structures.","tests":"Histology of the middle-ear lesion, culture if infection coexists, and imaging as appropriate.","ref":"Lecture 79 — Tympanokeratoma slide 20, otitis media slide 18, inflammatory polyp slide 21","options":[{"name":"Tympanokeratoma (aural cholesteatoma)","supports":"Older Cocker Spaniel, chronic otitis and keratin-filled bulla with cholesterol granulomatous reaction.","against":"Concurrent bacterial otitis media is possible and should not be missed.","hist":"Keratin debris with fibrovascular/granulomatous stroma and cholesterol crystals.","role":"best"},{"name":"Suppurative otitis media","supports":"Chronic history, head tilt and middle-ear expansion.","against":"Would expect tan-yellow mucoid/purulent inflammatory exudate rather than an organized keratinous cyst.","hist":"Neutrophilic exudate ± bacteria.","role":"less"},{"name":"Aural inflammatory polyp","supports":"Middle-ear mass can protrude toward canal or nasopharynx.","against":"Most often young cats with smooth off-white pedunculated fibrovascular mass, not an older dog with packed keratin cyst.","hist":"Inflamed fibrovascular core lined by respiratory/squamous epithelium.","role":"unlikely"},{"name":"Ceruminous gland adenocarcinoma","supports":"Neoplastic ear masses may be locally invasive.","against":"Ceruminous gland tumors primarily external ear canal, cats overrepresented; not a typical lamellated keratin bulla cyst.","hist":"Invasive glandular neoplasia.","role":"unlikely"}]},{"id":"n69-pem","lecture":"69","system":"Neuro","title":"Blind feedlot lamb: gray versus white matter","species":"6-month-old feedlot lamb","history":"After a rapid shift to a high-concentrate ration, a lamb becomes dull, develops cortical blindness and head-pressing, and then has seizures. No obvious ocular abnormality explains the blindness. The cerebral cortex is subtly swollen and yellow-gray; some gyri are flattened.","gross":"Bilaterally distributed cortical swelling, yellow discoloration and softening; chronic lesions may become cavitated. A Wood's lamp may demonstrate cortical autofluorescence, but absence does not exclude the disease.","histo":"Laminar neuronal necrosis in the cerebral gray matter, with neuronal eosinophilia and nuclear pyknosis, neuropil vacuolation, edema and variable macrophage reaction depending on duration.","best":"Polioencephalomalacia (cerebrocortical necrosis)","morph":"Cerebrum: bilateral, laminar, cortical neuronal necrosis (polioencephalomalacia)","mechanism":"Polioencephalomalacia describes the cortical lesion pattern, not one uniquely identified agent. In ruminants it can follow disruption of thiamine availability/metabolism or high sulfur exposure. Energy failure preferentially injures metabolically active cortical neurons.","tests":"Assess diet, ration transition, sulfur in water/feed and possible thiamine-related risk factors; evaluate response to treatment clinically. Histology establishes the lesion pattern but cannot distinguish sulfur-associated from thiamine-associated injury alone.","ref":"Lecture 69 — cortical necrosis/polioencephalomalacia topic; external veterinary diagnostic literature (confirm exact slide against lecture source)","options":[{"name":"Polioencephalomalacia (cerebrocortical necrosis)","supports":"Young ruminant, sudden blindness, ration change and bilaterally affected cerebral cortex strongly fit.","against":"Histology alone does not establish whether sulfur excess or thiamine disturbance was responsible.","hist":"Laminar cerebral cortical neuronal necrosis; gray-matter predominant.","role":"best"},{"name":"Fumonisin-associated equine leukoencephalomalacia","supports":"Seizures, blindness and cerebral malacia overlap.","against":"Equine disease with preferential cerebral white-matter damage; wrong species and tissue compartment.","hist":"Severe liquefactive WHITE-matter necrosis.","role":"unlikely"},{"name":"Lead toxicosis","supports":"Can cause acute cortical neurologic disease and blindness in ruminants.","against":"Requires a credible lead source and compatible systemic findings; cortical damage can overlap, so gross lesions alone are not decisive.","hist":"Cortical neuronal degeneration, edema and sometimes vascular lesions; lead confirmation relies on tissue/blood analysis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Salt poisoning / water deprivation","supports":"Can cause neurologic signs and cortical injury in some species.","against":"Strongly associated with pigs and relevant water/salt history; characteristic eosinophilic perivascular cuffing is more associated with porcine cases.","hist":"Laminar cortical neuronal injury, sometimes eosinophilic meningoencephalitis in pigs.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"n68-bse","lecture":"68","system":"Neuro","title":"Adult bovine: microscopic vacuoles and behavioral change","species":"5-year-old cow","history":"An adult cow develops a progressively abnormal temperament, hypersensitivity to sound and touch, incoordination and loss of condition. The brain appears grossly unremarkable; the teaching slide shows neuropil and neuronal vacuoles within selected brainstem nuclei.","gross":"No characteristic macroscopic CNS lesions despite progressive neurologic disease.","histo":"Bilateral symmetric spongiform vacuolation of neuropil, sometimes intraneuronal vacuoles, with neuronal loss and gliosis in characteristic brainstem regions. Distribution and validated testing are essential.","best":"Bovine spongiform encephalopathy (BSE)","morph":"Brainstem: spongiform encephalopathy (microscopic vacuolation)","mechanism":"Abnormally folded prion protein promotes misfolding and accumulation of host prion protein, leading to progressive neurodegeneration with little conventional inflammatory response.","tests":"Treat as a reportable prion-disease concern: contact the responsible regulatory veterinarian and diagnostic laboratory for approved collection and testing. Histologic vacuoles alone are not BSE-specific. Avoid unvalidated handling or testing claims.","ref":"Lecture 68 — spongiform encephalopathy/prion differential; USDA public-domain BSE histology reference (verify exact lecture slide alignment)","options":[{"name":"Bovine spongiform encephalopathy (BSE)","supports":"Adult bovine, progressive behavioral changes, ataxia and characteristic brainstem spongiform change support consideration.","against":"Cannot diagnose BSE on history or vacuolation alone; laboratory confirmation and exclusion of other causes are necessary.","hist":"Characteristic spongiform change involving selected brainstem nuclei with little inflammation.","role":"best"},{"name":"Rabies","supports":"Behavior change, hypersensitivity and neurologic dysfunction require consideration.","against":"Rabies generally features nonsuppurative encephalitis rather than the characteristic spongiform distribution.","hist":"Lymphocytic polioencephalitis, possible intracytoplasmic Negri bodies; confirmation uses validated assays.","role":"less"},{"name":"Toxic/metabolic vacuolar encephalopathy","supports":"Some toxicoses and metabolic conditions can cause neuropil/myelin vacuolation.","against":"Distribution, clinical course and ancillary evidence determine cause; vacuoles are not prion-specific.","hist":"Variable white matter or neuropil vacuolation and lesion distribution.","role":"less"},{"name":"Listeriosis","supports":"Common ruminant CNS infection can produce ataxia and cranial nerve deficits.","against":"Usually focal/asymmetric brainstem signs with suppurative microabscesses rather than noninflammatory spongiform change.","hist":"Suppurative microabscessing rhombencephalitis.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r76-granulosa","lecture":"76","system":"Female repro","title":"Mare with behavior change and one enlarged ovary","species":"11-year-old mare","history":"A mare has intermittent stallion-like behavior and failure to exhibit regular estrus. Rectal palpation and ultrasound identify one markedly enlarged multicystic ovary and a small, relatively inactive contralateral ovary. The ovarian cut surface is pale yellow-white with numerous cystic spaces.","gross":"Unilateral enlarged, smooth, solid-to-multicystic ovarian mass, yellow-white cut surface; contralateral ovarian atrophy.","histo":"Neoplastic granulosa cells in follicle-like microstructures (Call-Exner bodies may be present), cords or sheets, often with theca-cell proliferation; histologic architecture varies.","best":"Granulosa-theca cell tumor","morph":"Ovary: unilateral sex cord–stromal neoplasm (granulosa-theca cell tumor)","mechanism":"Functional neoplastic sex cord–stromal cells may produce inhibin and androgenic or estrogenic steroids, inhibiting follicle development in the opposite ovary and altering estrous or stallion-like behavior. Hormone patterns vary.","tests":"Ultrasonography, anti-Müllerian hormone and inhibin/testosterone where indicated; histopathology of removed ovary establishes subtype. Hormone concentrations are supportive, not substitutes for tissue diagnosis.","ref":"Lecture 76 catch-up PowerPoint, slides 3–4 speaker notes (ovarian tumor and granulosa-theca cell tumor)","options":[{"name":"Granulosa-theca cell tumor","supports":"Unilateral multicystic mass, contralateral atrophy and hormone-associated behavioral change fit.","against":"Some tumors are solid and not hormonally active; ultrasound alone cannot classify every ovarian mass.","hist":"Sex cord–stromal cords/follicular patterns; possible Call-Exner bodies.","role":"best"},{"name":"Persistent anovulatory follicle / follicular cyst","supports":"Ovarian cystic enlargement and abnormal cycling may overlap.","against":"Less convincing for a large mixed solid multicystic tumor with marked contralateral suppression and prolonged behavioral changes.","hist":"Cystic follicle lined by granulosa cells, without neoplastic sex cord proliferation.","role":"less"},{"name":"Ovarian epithelial cystadenoma","supports":"Ovarian epithelial tumors may form large multicystic masses.","against":"Typically lack the functional endocrine and contralateral atrophy pattern.","hist":"Cysts lined by neoplastic epithelial cells, sometimes papillary.","role":"less"},{"name":"Ovarian teratoma","supports":"Germ-cell neoplasms may enlarge an ovary.","against":"Expected heterogeneous tissue such as hair, bone or cartilage; endocrine effects less characteristic.","hist":"Differentiated tissues from multiple germ layers.","role":"unlikely"}]},{"id":"r76-pyometra","lecture":"76","system":"Female repro","title":"Intact dog with distended uterine horns","species":"9-year-old intact female dog","history":"Several weeks after estrus, a dog develops lethargy, polyuria/polydipsia and anorexia without visible vaginal discharge. Both uterine horns are markedly enlarged and fluid-filled. The endometrium bears numerous small cystic glands.","gross":"Tensely distended uterine horns containing thick yellow-green suppurative material; cystic endometrial glandular change. An apparently closed cervix can prevent vaginal discharge.","histo":"Neutrophilic luminal exudate, endometrial glandular cystic hyperplasia, variable mucosal necrosis and inflammatory extension depending on severity.","best":"Canine pyometra associated with cystic endometrial hyperplasia","morph":"Uterus: diffuse suppurative endometritis with luminal pus (pyometra), associated with cystic endometrial hyperplasia","mechanism":"Progesterone-dominant diestrus increases glandular secretion and reduces uterine contractility/clearance; ascending opportunistic bacteria, frequently E. coli, can establish infection and trigger endotoxemia.","tests":"Urgent systemic evaluation, imaging, CBC/chemistry and bacterial culture where appropriate; histopathology to assess inflammation and CEH. Clinical suspicion warrants timely stabilization and treatment.","ref":"Lecture 76 slides 17 and 21–24: CEH, metritis, canine pyometra and species distinctions","options":[{"name":"Canine pyometra associated with cystic endometrial hyperplasia","supports":"Diestrus, PU/PD, systemic illness, luminal pus, and neutrophilic endometritis.","against":"Gross purulence alone cannot identify bacterial species or degree of systemic compromise.","hist":"Suppurative endometritis with or without CEH.","role":"best"},{"name":"Mucometra","supports":"Distended uterus containing viscous material can mimic a fluid-filled pyometra on imaging.","against":"Sterile mucus without marked neutrophilic exudate or illness does not explain the suppurative findings.","hist":"Mucus-filled lumen with little/no suppurative inflammation.","role":"less"},{"name":"Hydrometra","supports":"Fluid-distended uterus could be similar grossly.","against":"Thin watery nonpurulent fluid and minimal uterine inflammation would favor hydrometra.","hist":"Watery luminal fluid without a major neutrophilic exudate.","role":"less"},{"name":"Postpartum metritis","supports":"Fetid uterine exudate and systemic bacterial illness are possible.","against":"Recent parturition or retained fetal membranes would usually be expected; metritis affects multiple uterine-wall layers.","hist":"Often more diffuse full-thickness uterine-wall inflammation.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r77-campy","lecture":"77","system":"Abortion","title":"Aborted lamb with distinctive liver lesions","species":"Late-gestation ewe and aborted fetus","history":"Several ewes abort late in gestation. One fetus has multiple approximately round pale target-like foci in the liver; the placenta is thickened and exudative. Other aborted fetuses have no obvious characteristic hepatic lesions.","gross":"Fetal liver: multifocal targetoid pale necrotic foci, sometimes with red or raised margins. Placenta may be edematous, necrotic and covered in exudate.","histo":"Suppurative to necrotizing placentitis, placental bacterial microcolonies, sometimes vasculitis, fetal pneumonia and random necrosuppurative hepatitis.","best":"Ovine Campylobacter-associated abortion (C. jejuni or C. fetus subsp. fetus)","morph":"Placenta: necrosuppurative placentitis; fetus: multifocal necrotizing hepatitis, consistent with bacterial abortion","mechanism":"Maternal intestinal exposure and bacteremia permit placental colonization and fetal infection; a compromised exchange surface and direct fetal infection lead to abortion. Target-like hepatic lesions are strongly suggestive but not pathognomonic.","tests":"Submit fresh placenta, fetal abomasal contents, lung and liver for appropriate culture/PCR (laboratory guidance); fixed placenta and fetal organs for histology. Observe zoonotic abortion-tissue precautions.","ref":"Lecture 77 PowerPoint slides 36–38 speaker notes (Campylobacter species and target lesions)","options":[{"name":"Ovine Campylobacter-associated abortion (C. jejuni or C. fetus subsp. fetus)","supports":"Late abortion with multifocal targetoid fetal liver necrosis and necrosuppurative placentitis is a high-yield pattern.","against":"Characteristic liver foci are inconsistent among cases and are not sufficient to identify the species.","hist":"Bacterial placentitis with microcolonies; fetal necrosuppurative hepatitis and pneumonia.","role":"best"},{"name":"Chlamydia abortus","supports":"Late ewe abortion with severe placentitis and little fetal pathology is common.","against":"Typically striking intercotyledonary leathery change and dirty red-brown exudate rather than targetoid fetal liver lesions.","hist":"Necrotizing placentitis, sometimes vasculitis; organism requires specific testing.","role":"less"},{"name":"Coxiella burnetii","supports":"Ruminant abortion with severe placentitis, possibly with few fetal lesions.","against":"Fetal targetoid hepatic foci are less characteristic, and many cases have limited fetal changes.","hist":"Severe trophoblast-rich necrotizing placentitis; intracellular bacteria may be abundant.","role":"less"},{"name":"Brucella melitensis","supports":"Zoonotic ovine/caprine late abortion with placentitis and fetal pneumonia.","against":"No unique liver target signature; laboratory confirmation and public health precautions are necessary.","hist":"Necrotizing placentitis and fetal bronchopneumonia.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r77-ehv","lecture":"77","system":"Abortion","title":"Sudden third-trimester abortion in a mare","species":"Pregnant mare, approximately month 10","history":"A mare with no obvious preceding fever or reproductive discharge aborts a relatively fresh fetus. The fetal lungs do not fully collapse, are heavy and edematous; the liver has multiple small pale foci, and the thymus may show petechiae. The placenta has no dominant cervical-star exudative lesion.","gross":"Fresh fetus with pulmonary edema and small pale foci in liver and/or lungs, possible body-cavity fluid and petechiae; gross findings may be mild or absent.","histo":"Multifocal hepatic necrosis with intranuclear viral inclusions in hepatocytes and sometimes other affected cells; pulmonary and lymphoid tissue injury can occur.","best":"Equine herpesvirus 1 abortion","morph":"Fetus: multifocal hepatic necrosis and pulmonary edema, consistent with viral fetal infection","mechanism":"Maternal EHV-1 infection can produce cell-associated viremia and fetal/placental infection late in gestation; fetal systemic infection and tissue necrosis follow, often without warning to the dam.","tests":"PCR on appropriate fetal lung, liver, thymus and placenta; histopathology and diagnostic laboratory guidance. Apply farm biosecurity during abortion investigation.","ref":"Lecture 76 slide 34 (EHV-1 abortion); Lecture 77 fetal abortion investigation slides 26–29","options":[{"name":"Equine herpesvirus 1 abortion","supports":"Abrupt late abortion, fresh fetus, pulmonary edema and multifocal hepatic necrosis strongly support.","against":"Some EHV-1 abortions lack distinct lesions; PCR is needed to confirm.","hist":"Hepatic necrosis with possible eosinophilic intranuclear inclusions.","role":"best"},{"name":"Ascending bacterial placentitis","supports":"Common cause of equine abortion and fetal infection.","against":"Usually suppurative placentitis prominent at cervical star; fetal liver viral-type inclusions would not fit.","hist":"Neutrophilic chorioallantoitis with bacteria; fetal bronchopneumonia possible.","role":"less"},{"name":"Leptospiral abortion","supports":"Can cause late equine abortion with placental edema and fetal liver lesions.","against":"Often requires renal/placental pathogen evidence; typical viral intranuclear inclusions not explained.","hist":"Variable nephritis, hepatitis and placentitis without viral inclusions.","role":"less"},{"name":"Umbilical cord torsion","supports":"May cause sudden noninfectious fetal hypoxia and death.","against":"Cord twisting/vascular compromise should be apparent; does not explain viral inclusion-bearing hepatic necrosis.","hist":"Hypoxic/vascular tissue injury, no infectious cytopathic inclusions.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r77-mummy","lecture":"77","system":"Abortion","title":"Small retained fetal remains: mummy or maceration?","species":"Multiparous sow","history":"A sow farrows several healthy piglets with two much smaller dark, desiccated, intact fetal forms. These have leathery skin and are not foul-smelling. No purulent uterine fluid is evident. Determine the process before assuming an infectious cause.","gross":"Shrunken dry brown-black fetal remnants maintaining fetal outline, without gas, pus or marked decomposition.","histo":"Extensive postmortem dehydration and autolysis; little diagnostic intact fetal histology is expected. Absence of purulent uterine inflammation supports an aseptic retention process.","best":"Fetal mummification","morph":"Uterine contents: retained desiccated fetuses (mummification)","mechanism":"Fetal death after sufficient skin development and retention in a relatively sterile uterine environment permits water resorption while a continuing pregnancy or luteal function prevents immediate expulsion. The morphology is an outcome, not an etiologic diagnosis.","tests":"Assess gestational stage, sizes and count of affected fetuses, placenta, sow reproductive history and herd pattern. In swine consider porcine parvovirus among causes of variable-size mummies; determine etiology separately by appropriate fetal/placental testing.","ref":"Lecture 77 catch-up PowerPoint slides 16–20 speaker notes (mummification, maceration, emphysematous fetus); Lecture 76 slide 29 PPV/SMEDI","options":[{"name":"Fetal mummification","supports":"Sterile-appearing, dark leathery, intact dry fetus in mixed-size litter.","against":"Cannot establish which infection or noninfectious event caused fetal death.","hist":"Desiccated autolyzed retained fetal tissues.","role":"best"},{"name":"Fetal maceration","supports":"Retained dead fetal tissue could also break down over time.","against":"Maceration generally involves bacterial infection, fetal tissue disintegration and pus, often with retained bones.","hist":"Suppurative uterine inflammation and decomposed fetal remains.","role":"less"},{"name":"Emphysematous fetus","supports":"Dead retained fetus may become infected.","against":"Would expect gas-producing putrefaction, swelling, crepitation and malodor, not dry intact tissues.","hist":"Putrefactive tissue changes with bacterial overgrowth.","role":"unlikely"},{"name":"Normal stillbirth","supports":"Perinatal fetal death can accompany a live-born litter.","against":"Normal-size fresh stillborn fetus does not explain chronically retained, small dry fetal remnants.","hist":"Variable findings depending on death timing; not characteristic dry mummification.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r77-toxo","lecture":"77","system":"Abortion","title":"White cotyledonary foci and fetal brain necrosis","species":"Ewe with late-pregnancy loss","history":"An ewe aborts in late gestation. Several cotyledons have small well-demarcated pale spots; intercotyledonary membranes are not uniformly leathery. Histology has yet to be interpreted. A regional veterinarian is investigating the flock's fetal losses.","gross":"Multiple pale pinpoint-to-millimeter necrotic/mineralized foci centered on placental cotyledons; fetal gross lesions may be absent.","histo":"Multifocal necrotizing placentitis with protozoal organisms in trophoblasts; fetal nonsuppurative necrotizing encephalitis can be present. Demonstration of parasite antigen/DNA strengthens etiologic attribution.","best":"Toxoplasma gondii-associated ovine abortion","morph":"Placenta: multifocal necrotizing cotyledonary placentitis; fetus: multifocal necrotizing encephalitis","mechanism":"Maternal ingestion of sporulated feline-origin oocysts permits parasitemia and transplacental infection; infected trophoblast and fetal tissues develop necrosis. Fetal outcome depends in part on gestation stage.","tests":"Placental and fetal brain PCR plus histology and immunohistochemistry where available; maternal serology alone does not prove abortion etiology. Protect pregnant/immunocompromised handlers from fetal and placental tissues.","ref":"Lecture 77 abortion and placentitis differential; Dorsch et al. 2022, Front Vet Sci Figure 3 (open-access CC BY diagnostic case)","options":[{"name":"Toxoplasma gondii-associated ovine abortion","supports":"Cotyledonary white foci and fetal necrotizing encephalitis fit; placental histology can demonstrate protozoa.","against":"Cotyledonary pallor alone is not diagnostic; need specific tissue confirmation.","hist":"Focal cotyledonary trophoblast necrosis with protozoal organisms; fetal necrotizing encephalitis.","role":"best"},{"name":"Campylobacter-associated ovine abortion","supports":"Late abortion and necrotizing placentitis are compatible.","against":"More characteristic fetal targetoid liver foci and bacterial placental microcolonies; do not confuse these with white cotyledon foci.","hist":"Necrosuppurative placentitis with bacterial microcolonies; fetal hepatitis/pneumonia.","role":"less"},{"name":"Chlamydia abortus","supports":"Late ewe abortion with placentitis.","against":"More typically extensive dirty red-brown leathery intercotyledonary chorioallantoitis.","hist":"Necrotizing placentitis, with specific agent demonstrated by PCR/IHC.","role":"less"},{"name":"Coxiella burnetii","supports":"Marked ruminant placentitis with little fetal injury can occur.","against":"Cotyledonary pale foci plus fetal necrotizing encephalitis are not its strongest pattern.","hist":"Severe necrotizing placentitis, often numerous intracellular bacteria in trophoblasts.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r75-ram-epididymis","lecture":"75","system":"Male repro","title":"Ram with a firm unilateral epididymal tail","species":"3-year-old breeding ram","history":"At breeding soundness examination the ram has decreased fertility and a hard enlarged cauda epididymidis on one side. A cut section shows fibrous thickening, focal creamy debris and distended ducts containing inspissated sperm. Some affected rams in the flock have no palpable changes.","gross":"Firm enlarged epididymal tail, fibrosis and multifocal abscess-like or sperm-granulomatous foci; testis may be atrophic.","histo":"Chronic interstitial lymphoplasmacytic and suppurative epididymitis, duct obstruction, sperm leakage and foreign-body granulomatous reaction; bacterial identification requires ancillary testing.","best":"Brucella ovis-associated epididymitis","morph":"Epididymis: chronic active, fibrosing, sperm-granulomatous epididymitis","mechanism":"B. ovis establishes chronic reproductive-tract infection; duct inflammation and obstruction rupture sperm-containing ducts, exposing sperm antigens to the interstitium and generating granulomatous inflammation, fibrosis and impaired sperm transport.","tests":"Brucella ovis flock testing/serology and organism-specific PCR or culture under diagnostic laboratory guidance. Palpation alone misses subclinical carriers; local reporting and herd policies vary.","ref":"Lecture 75 slides 19–20 (ram epididymitis: Brucella ovis versus Actinobacillus seminis)","options":[{"name":"Brucella ovis-associated epididymitis","supports":"Sexually mature ram, characteristic cauda epididymis involvement, flock infertility and possible subclinical carriers.","against":"Clinical and gross appearance alone cannot distinguish all bacterial causes.","hist":"Chronic active epididymitis with periductal fibrosis and sperm granulomas.","role":"best"},{"name":"Actinobacillus seminis epididymitis","supports":"Can cause pyogranulomatous epididymitis and infertility in rams.","against":"More often affects younger rams; significant overlap means culture/PCR is decisive.","hist":"Suppurative/pyogranulomatous epididymitis, not etiologically distinct by H&E alone.","role":"less"},{"name":"Noninfectious sperm granuloma after trauma","supports":"Ruptured ducts release sperm and generate granulomatous reaction.","against":"Flock-associated epididymitis and infectious inflammation increase suspicion of infection.","hist":"Foreign-body giant-cell inflammation around extravasated sperm, with little bacterial evidence.","role":"less"},{"name":"Testicular seminoma","supports":"May cause a firm gonadal mass and infertility.","against":"Primary lesion is epididymal rather than a discrete intratesticular germ-cell tumor.","hist":"Sheets of neoplastic germ cells within testis.","role":"unlikely"}]},{"id":"r75-prostate","lecture":"75","system":"Male repro","title":"Older dog with pelvic outlet obstruction and bone pain","species":"11-year-old neutered male dog","history":"A dog has tenesmus, dysuria, progressive weight loss, and reluctance to walk. Rectal examination reveals an irregular firm fixed prostate; imaging shows mineralization, sublumbar lymphadenopathy and new proliferative lesions of lumbar vertebrae.","gross":"Irregular infiltrative mineralized prostate, sometimes asymmetrical, with firm adjacent tissue and regional lymph node enlargement.","histo":"Infiltrative malignant epithelial cells in irregular tubules, nests or solid sheets, marked atypia and stromal desmoplasia. Tumor type and origin may require immunohistochemistry.","best":"Canine prostatic carcinoma","morph":"Prostate: infiltrative malignant epithelial neoplasm (prostatic carcinoma)","mechanism":"Malignant prostatic epithelium or urothelial-type carcinoma infiltrates local structures, obstructs urinary/fecal passage and may metastasize to nodes, lungs and axial skeleton.","tests":"Imaging and tissue/cytology assessment with histopathology and immunohistochemistry as needed; distinguish primary prostatic from urothelial carcinoma. Metastasis staging is clinically important.","ref":"Lecture 75 slides 28–31 (prostatic cysts, prostatitis, benign hyperplasia and carcinoma)","options":[{"name":"Canine prostatic carcinoma","supports":"Firm irregular mineralized prostate, neutered status and possible axial skeletal metastases.","against":"Prostatic mineralization and inflammation can overlap; obtain tissue characterization.","hist":"Infiltrating atypical glandular/urothelial-like epithelial cells.","role":"best"},{"name":"Benign prostatic hyperplasia","supports":"Older male dog with prostatomegaly and straining.","against":"Usually intact, smoother and symmetric enlargement; infiltrative fixed lesion plus metastases does not fit.","hist":"Orderly hyperplastic glands with epithelial proliferation and minimal invasion.","role":"less"},{"name":"Prostatic abscess / bacterial prostatitis","supports":"Can cause painful enlargement, focal cavities and dysuria.","against":"Fever, infection evidence and purulent exudate would support infection; osseous lesions are not typical metastases from abscess.","hist":"Suppurative inflammation and destruction, sometimes bacterial colonies.","role":"less"},{"name":"Paraprostatic cyst","supports":"May cause marked pelvic mass effect and straining.","against":"Usually an extraprostatic cystic structure, rather than fixed mineralized infiltrative gland.","hist":"Cyst wall with variable lining, not infiltrative malignant glands.","role":"unlikely"}]},{"id":"r76-mastitis","lecture":"76","system":"Mammary","title":"Sudden agalactia versus gangrenous mastitis","species":"Postpartum dairy cow","history":"A cow has sudden severe depression, fever, and greatly reduced milk production in one quarter. The secretion is thin, watery and blood tinged. The affected gland is markedly swollen and edematous, with sharply demarcated dusky necrotic areas and systemic signs of endotoxemia.","gross":"Marked edema and hemorrhage, necrotic mammary tissue and watery abnormal milk from one involved quarter.","histo":"Severe neutrophilic and necrotizing mastitis, with vascular injury and edema; histology alone does not reliably identify an individual coliform species.","best":"Acute severe coliform (E. coli) mastitis","morph":"Mammary gland: acute severe necrosuppurative and hemorrhagic mastitis","mechanism":"Ascending intramammary Gram-negative bacterial infection activates powerful innate inflammatory responses, including LPS-driven systemic inflammatory and vascular effects that damage the gland and cause shock.","tests":"Aseptically collected milk culture/PCR as appropriate, assessment for systemic inflammatory response and shock, fluid/electrolyte status, and clinical management. Necrosis pattern is not organism-specific.","ref":"Lecture 76 slides 37–42 (coliform, staphylococcal, streptococcal, and Mycoplasma mastitis)","options":[{"name":"Acute severe coliform (E. coli) mastitis","supports":"Peracute severe systemic illness, one affected quarter, watery/bloody secretion and necrotizing edema.","against":"Staphylococcal gangrene can look very similar; culture needed.","hist":"Acute necrotizing neutrophilic mastitis and marked edema/vascular injury.","role":"best"},{"name":"Staphylococcus aureus gangrenous mastitis","supports":"Can cause gangrenous blue-black mammary necrosis and severe toxemia.","against":"Clinical pattern and histology overlap; culture rather than gross color proves agent.","hist":"Necrosuppurative mastitis and often coccal aggregates.","role":"less"},{"name":"Mycoplasma bovis mastitis","supports":"Agalactia and rapidly affected herd mates are typical.","against":"Often multiple quarters, firm swollen udder and floccular milk with less overt severe endotoxemic crisis.","hist":"Variable suppurative mastitis; confirmation with specific molecular testing/culture.","role":"less"},{"name":"Streptococcal mastitis","supports":"Milk quality changes and inflamed quarter are plausible.","against":"Often less striking vascular necrosis and shock in comparison; overlap possible.","hist":"Neutrophilic mastitis with bacteria, requiring organism testing.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r77-mycotic","lecture":"77","system":"Abortion","title":"Leathery bovine placenta with fetal skin plaques","species":"Dairy cow, last third of pregnancy","history":"A cow aborts a late-gestation fetus. The placenta has thick, opaque, leathery intercotyledonary membranes and enlarged necrotic cotyledons. Raised gray-white crusting lesions are present on the fetal head and neck. A section of placenta reveals abundant branching hyphae invading chorionic tissue.","gross":"Necrotizing and thickened leathery placenta, sometimes adventitial placentation, with focal gray-white raised fetal skin plaques and possible pneumonia.","histo":"Necrotizing placentitis with invading fungal hyphae; septate acute-angle branching hyphae favor Aspergillus, whereas broader pauciseptate hyphae favor certain Mucorales. Morphology should be confirmed by culture or molecular methods.","best":"Mycotic abortion, most suggestive of Aspergillus spp.","morph":"Placenta: severe necrotizing fungal placentitis with fetal mycotic dermatitis","mechanism":"Environmental fungi enter maternal circulation and colonize placentomes, spread through fetal membranes and impair placental exchange; placental and fetal infection cause fetal death. Exact fungal species cannot be established by gross morphology alone.","tests":"Submit fresh and fixed placenta, fetal skin lesions, lung and stomach contents; perform fungal culture/PCR, H&E, PAS and GMS stains. Species identification requires laboratory support.","ref":"Lecture 77 slide 44 speaker notes (Aspergillus-associated bovine abortion); veterinary diagnostic pathology literature","options":[{"name":"Mycotic abortion, most suggestive of Aspergillus spp.","supports":"Leathery intercotyledonary placentitis plus fetal fungal dermatitis and invading septate hyphae.","against":"A mold species must not be named from gross appearance alone; Mucorales differ microscopically.","hist":"Fungal invasion of placental stroma/vessels; septate hyphae on special stain.","role":"best"},{"name":"Brucella abortus abortion","supports":"Necrotizing placentitis and fetal pneumonia occur.","against":"Fungal hyphae in placenta and fetal skin favor mycotic etiology; brucellosis requires specific testing.","hist":"Necrosuppurative placentitis with bacteria rather than true tissue-invasive fungal hyphae.","role":"less"},{"name":"Neospora caninum abortion","supports":"Important bovine transplacental cause of late abortion.","against":"Usually fetal nonsuppurative encephalitis and myocarditis, not dense fungal placentitis and cutaneous plaques.","hist":"Necrotizing nonsuppurative encephalitis and myocarditis with protozoal organisms.","role":"less"},{"name":"Chlamydial placentitis","supports":"Necrotizing placentitis can occur in ruminants.","against":"Does not produce tissue-invasive branching hyphae.","hist":"Trophoblast necrosis/inflammation with intracellular bacteria, not fungi.","role":"unlikely"}]},{"id":"r77-neospora","lecture":"77","system":"Abortion","title":"Bovine fetus with encephalitis but few gross lesions","species":"Cow aborting at 6 months gestation","history":"A beef herd experiences several mid-gestation abortions and repeat losses among certain dam lines. The fetuses are often autolyzed with no dramatic external lesions. Fetal brain and heart histology shows scattered areas of necrosis and mononuclear inflammation.","gross":"Often no distinctive gross fetal lesion; autolysis can obscure findings. Occasionally focal pale tissue changes.","histo":"Multifocal necrotizing nonsuppurative encephalitis and myocarditis, often with glial response; intralesional protozoal cysts/tachyzoites may be sparse.","best":"Neospora caninum-associated bovine abortion","morph":"Fetus: multifocal necrotizing nonsuppurative encephalitis and myocarditis","mechanism":"Transplacental Neospora infection, including endogenous reactivation in persistently infected dams, injures fetal neural and cardiac tissues; vertical transmission explains recurrence in dam lines.","tests":"Fetal brain/heart histopathology with Neospora PCR and immunohistochemistry where available; dam serology informs herd risk but does not alone establish causation.","ref":"Lecture 77 slide 46 speaker notes (Neospora caninum bovine abortion)","options":[{"name":"Neospora caninum-associated bovine abortion","supports":"Mid-gestation cattle abortions, recurrence in dam lines, fetal nonsuppurative encephalitis and myocarditis.","against":"Protozoal morphology may be sparse and Toxoplasma/other etiologies can overlap; confirm by specific tests.","hist":"Multifocal necrotizing nonsuppurative encephalitis and myocarditis.","role":"best"},{"name":"Bovine viral diarrhea virus fetal infection","supports":"Can cause fetal loss and neurologic malformations.","against":"Hydranencephaly, cerebellar hypoplasia or ocular lesions depend on infection timing; necrotizing myocarditis plus protozoal encephalitis is more suggestive of Neospora.","hist":"Developmental CNS abnormalities or nonspecific inflammatory/necrotic changes.","role":"less"},{"name":"Bovine herpesvirus 1 abortion","supports":"Can produce fetal hepatic necrosis and abortion.","against":"Hepatic multifocal necrosis, sometimes viral intranuclear inclusions, more typical than neural/cardiac protozoal pattern.","hist":"Fetal hepatocellular necrosis with possible intranuclear inclusions.","role":"less"},{"name":"Toxoplasma gondii","supports":"Related protozoan can cause fetal necrotizing encephalitis.","against":"Ovine/caprine abortion more characteristic; bovine abortion due to Toxoplasma is uncommon, species testing essential.","hist":"Protozoal-associated necrotizing encephalitis; immunostains/PCR differentiate.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r76-teratoma","lecture":"76","system":"Female repro","title":"Ovarian mass with hair and mineralized material","species":"4-year-old intact female dog","history":"Exploratory surgery for abdominal distention reveals one enlarged ovarian mass. Its cut surface contains cysts filled with greasy keratinous material and hair, interspersed with firm white mineralized fragments. The other ovary is grossly normal.","gross":"Heterogeneous unilateral ovarian mass with hair, sebum, cysts and bone/cartilage-like material, a strong but not pathognomonic gross pattern.","histo":"Well-differentiated tissue derivatives from at least two germ layers, potentially cutaneous adnexa, cartilage, bone, adipose, neural, or glandular/respiratory-like epithelium.","best":"Mature ovarian teratoma","morph":"Ovary: mature germ-cell teratoma with multiple differentiated tissue types","mechanism":"A totipotent ovarian germ cell differentiates aberrantly into multiple somatic tissue lineages. A mature teratoma may be benign yet can exert mass effects; malignant potential requires histopathologic assessment.","tests":"Submit representative sections of solid and cystic regions for histopathology; inspect for immature or malignant components and evaluate local extension. Gross tissue diversity strongly directs the differential but alone does not grade behavior.","ref":"Lecture 76 catch-up PPT slide 3 and slide 6 speaker notes; original lecture 76 slide 52 (ovarian teratoma)","options":[{"name":"Mature ovarian teratoma","supports":"Hair, sebum and cartilage/bone within an ovarian tumor strongly favor divergent germ-layer tissue differentiation.","against":"Gross findings cannot exclude an immature/malignant component; adequate histologic sampling is essential.","hist":"Differentiated tissues representing at least two embryonic germ layers.","role":"best"},{"name":"Granulosa-theca cell tumor","supports":"Ovarian mass may contain many cysts and appear yellow-white.","against":"Hair and cartilage are not explained by sex cord–stromal neoplasia; associated endocrine effects may favor granulosa-theca.","hist":"Granulosa-cell rosettes/cords and variable thecal cells, no ectopic mature cartilage/hair.","role":"less"},{"name":"Ovarian epithelial cystadenoma","supports":"Cystic epithelial tumor may become large and multilocular.","against":"Cyst walls are epithelial rather than containing hair follicles and bone.","hist":"Cysts lined by relatively orderly epithelial cells with possible papillae.","role":"less"},{"name":"Ovarian dysgerminoma","supports":"Germ-cell origin and unilateral ovarian enlargement overlap.","against":"Usually soft solid gray-white mass rather than differentiated hair/bone-containing cysts.","hist":"Sheets of primitive germ cells, lacking mature mixed tissue types.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r76-ovarian-epithelial","lecture":"76","system":"Female repro","title":"Ovarian papillary mass with abdominal implants","species":"8-year-old intact female dog","history":"An intact female dog has progressive abdominal distention and ascites. A complex ovarian mass has numerous delicate papillary projections extending through a disrupted bursal surface, with small similar nodules on peritoneal surfaces.","gross":"Irregular ovarian papillary/cystic mass with peritoneal implants and effusion; assess the ovarian bursa and serosal distribution.","histo":"Malignant ovarian epithelial cells line complex papillary fronds, with pleomorphism, mitoses, stromal invasion and possible peritoneal implants.","best":"Ovarian papillary cystadenocarcinoma","morph":"Ovary: malignant papillary epithelial neoplasm with transcoelomic spread","mechanism":"Malignant surface/subsurface epithelial neoplasia invades the ovarian capsule/bursa and sheds cells throughout the peritoneal cavity, causing implants and sometimes effusion.","tests":"Histopathology of ovarian mass and peritoneal deposits; sample effusion and stage involvement of abdomen. Papillary gross morphology alone does not establish invasion.","ref":"Lecture 76 catch-up PPT slide 5 speaker notes; original Lecture 76 slide 51","options":[{"name":"Ovarian papillary cystadenocarcinoma","supports":"Papillary epithelial mass, disrupted bursal boundary and peritoneal implants support malignant epithelial spread.","against":"Determine exact epithelial origin and invasion histologically; other peritoneal malignancies are possible.","hist":"Invasive atypical epithelial papillae with metastatic implants.","role":"best"},{"name":"Ovarian papillary cystadenoma","supports":"Papillary cystic architecture is compatible.","against":"Benign adenoma should not invade or produce malignant serosal implants.","hist":"Orderly papillae without stromal invasion.","role":"less"},{"name":"Granulosa-theca cell tumor","supports":"Large ovarian neoplasm may have cystic spaces and ascites.","against":"Papillary epithelial lining and peritoneal epithelial implants favor carcinoma.","hist":"Sex-cord stromal cellular patterns, sometimes Call-Exner bodies.","role":"less"},{"name":"Primary peritoneal mesothelioma","supports":"Serosal nodules and effusion may mimic disseminated ovarian carcinoma.","against":"Dominant ovarian primary and epithelial ovarian architecture support ovarian origin.","hist":"Mesothelial growth patterns; immunohistochemistry may be necessary.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r75-equine-scc","lecture":"75","system":"Male repro","title":"Older gelding with ulcerated penile mass","species":"18-year-old gelding","history":"An older gelding has recurrent blood-tinged preputial discharge and difficulty retracting the penis. A firm ulcerated irregular plaque and nodular mass occupy the glans; regional inguinal nodes are mildly enlarged.","gross":"Firm infiltrative ulcerated proliferative penile/preputial mass, potentially extending into deeper tissues.","histo":"Invasive islands and cords of atypical squamous epithelium penetrate the stroma; variable keratin pearls, intercellular bridges, desmoplasia and mitoses.","best":"Equine penile squamous cell carcinoma","morph":"Penis: infiltrative keratinizing squamous cell carcinoma","mechanism":"Malignant transformation of squamous epithelium produces local invasion and may involve equine papillomavirus 2; persistent genital papillomatous lesions can be a risk context, but viral DNA does not establish causation for an individual tumor.","tests":"Multiple biopsies including lesion edges and invasive base; assess penile depth, inguinal lymph nodes and metastatic spread. Histology determines invasive versus in situ disease.","ref":"Lecture 75 slides 35–37 (penile tumors and EcPV2-associated equine SCC)","options":[{"name":"Equine penile squamous cell carcinoma","supports":"Older horse with firm ulcerative invasive glans lesion and enlarged nodes.","against":"Biopsy needed to show invasion; papilloma and carcinoma in situ may look similar grossly.","hist":"Invasive atypical squamous nests with keratinization/desmoplasia.","role":"best"},{"name":"Squamous papilloma","supports":"Exophytic genital squamous growth can occur and may be EcPV associated.","against":"Typical benign papilloma remains exophytic without invasive nests in deeper connective tissue.","hist":"Papillary fronds with fibrovascular cores and orderly squamous epithelium.","role":"less"},{"name":"Bovine papillavirus-type fibropapilloma","supports":"Cauliflower-like genital masses can be confusing.","against":"Classically young bulls rather than aged geldings, with a prominent fibrous stromal core.","hist":"Papillomatous epithelial hyperplasia over fibroblastic stroma.","role":"unlikely"},{"name":"Equine sarcoid","supports":"Fibroblastic cutaneous mass can involve preputial skin.","against":"Histology dominated by dermal spindle-cell fibroblastic proliferation without invasive malignant squamous islands.","hist":"Dermal fibroblast proliferation with overlying epidermal changes.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r75-tvt","lecture":"75","system":"Male repro","title":"Bleeding cauliflower-like penile mass in a dog","species":"3-year-old intact male free-roaming dog","history":"The dog has sanguineous preputial discharge and a friable red lobulated mass at the cranial glans. Cytology shows many discrete round cells with pale cytoplasmic vacuoles, prominent nuclei and mitotic figures.","gross":"Soft pink-red multilobulated friable mucosal mass that bleeds readily; may have ulceration.","histo":"Sheets of round neoplastic cells with distinct cell borders, round nuclei, variably vacuolated cytoplasm, high cellularity and frequent mitoses, with thin fibrovascular septa.","best":"Canine transmissible venereal tumor (TVT)","morph":"Penis: transmissible round-cell neoplasm (TVT)","mechanism":"TVT is a clonally transmissible cancer in which living tumor cells implant during genital contact or close contact with affected lesions; it is not a conventional viral papilloma.","tests":"Fine-needle aspiration or impression cytology often diagnostic; histopathology when needed and assess extragenital lesions and lymph nodes. Distinguish from lymphoma and other round-cell tumors using cytology and appropriate testing.","ref":"Lecture 75 slide 38 (canine TVT); diagnostic textbook TVT histology/cytology references","options":[{"name":"Canine transmissible venereal tumor (TVT)","supports":"Sexual/contact history, friable genital mass and vacuolated round cells match TVT.","against":"Round-cell cytology can overlap with other tumors; verify representative features.","hist":"Round neoplastic cells with cytoplasmic vacuoles and mitoses.","role":"best"},{"name":"Canine genital papilloma","supports":"Genital exophytic mass can have cauliflower appearance.","against":"Papilloma is an epithelial frond rather than sheets of discrete vacuolated round cells.","hist":"Papillary stratified squamous epithelium over fibrovascular cores.","role":"less"},{"name":"Lymphoma","supports":"A genital extranodal round-cell tumor is possible.","against":"TVT-type cytoplasmic vacuolation and typical location strongly favor TVT; lymphocyte phenotype requires testing.","hist":"Monomorphic atypical lymphoid cells without classic TVT vacuolation.","role":"less"},{"name":"Squamous cell carcinoma","supports":"Ulcerated bleeding penile mass is possible.","against":"Cohesive malignant squamous cells/keratinization rather than discrete vacuolated round cells.","hist":"Invasive squamous epithelial nests and keratinization.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r77-coxiella","lecture":"77","system":"Abortion","title":"Abortion storm with leathery placenta and minimal fetal lesions","species":"Goat herd in late gestation","history":"Several does abort late in gestation. Fetuses are fresh or mildly autolyzed with no distinctive lesions. The placenta is thick, leathery, and coated by abundant off-white exudate, especially between placentomes. Laboratory microscopy shows numerous small intracellular coccobacilli in distended trophoblasts.","gross":"Thickened opaque chorioallantois with abundant cream/off-white intercotyledonary exudate and possible placental mineralization, sometimes without specific fetal lesions.","histo":"Severe necrotizing placentitis, with trophoblasts swollen by abundant intracellular small Gram-negative coccobacilli; confirm with immunohistochemistry/PCR due to overlapping agents.","best":"Coxiella burnetii-associated placentitis (Q fever)","morph":"Placenta: severe diffuse necrotizing placentitis with intracellular bacteria","mechanism":"C. burnetii replicates in placental trophoblasts, leading to high organism burden, necrosis and abortion. Infected placentas and aerosols create a substantial zoonotic risk, including for pregnant people.","tests":"Coordinate diagnostic sampling with the laboratory; placental PCR/IHC plus histology and herd epidemiology. Avoid aerosols, use appropriate protective measures and notify public/animal health authorities as required.","ref":"Lecture 77 slide 42 speaker notes (Q fever); published bovine and caprine pathology literature","options":[{"name":"Coxiella burnetii-associated placentitis (Q fever)","supports":"Placenta-predominant late abortion with profound trophoblast infection and thick exudate fits.","against":"Similar gross placentitis occurs with Chlamydia and others; confirm organism by specific tests.","hist":"Necrotizing placentitis with numerous intracellular organisms in trophoblasts.","role":"best"},{"name":"Chlamydia abortus (enzootic abortion)","supports":"Late small-ruminant abortion with leathery intercotyledonary necrotizing placentitis strongly overlaps.","against":"Gross alone usually cannot separate these; PCR/IHC and microbe-specific testing is needed.","hist":"Necrotizing placentitis with intracellular obligate bacteria; histology can overlap.","role":"less"},{"name":"Brucella melitensis","supports":"Caprine late abortion with necrotizing placentitis and zoonotic hazard.","against":"Fetal pneumonia and different organism testing may favor brucellosis; gross overlap remains substantial.","hist":"Necrosuppurative placentitis with bacteria, possibly fetal bronchopneumonia.","role":"less"},{"name":"Toxoplasma gondii","supports":"Small-ruminant abortion and placental necrosis can occur.","against":"More typical multifocal pale cotyledon foci and fetal encephalitis than extensive bacteria-laden intercotyledonary placentitis.","hist":"Protozoal-associated cotyledonary necrosis and fetal necrotizing encephalitis.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r77-trich","lecture":"77","system":"Abortion","title":"Repeat-breeder cattle herd with infected bulls","species":"Beef cattle herd, natural service","history":"Several cows repeatedly return to estrus after breeding and a minority develop pyometra or early abortion. Older herd bulls appear clinically normal, but preputial samples from one bull contain motile flagellated protozoa by a laboratory-validated assay.","gross":"Usually minimal specific gross lesions; cows may have mild cervicitis, endometritis, uterine pus or variable placental thickening. Bulls often have no visible changes.","histo":"Mild-to-moderate lymphoplasmacytic or mixed inflammatory endometritis/placentitis; organisms may be difficult to detect histologically and microscopic findings are nonspecific.","best":"Tritrichomonas foetus-associated bovine trichomoniasis","morph":"Uterus: variable endometritis/pyometra secondary to venereally transmitted protozoal infection","mechanism":"T. foetus colonizes the preputial crypts of carrier bulls and spreads during mating. In cows genital infection causes early embryonic death, repeat breeding and occasionally pyometra or abortion.","tests":"Validated PCR/culture on appropriately collected bull preputial material or female genital samples; follow state herd disease-control and testing requirements. Microscopic motility alone should not stand in for species identification.","ref":"Lecture 77 slides 47–48 speaker notes (Tritrichomonas foetus cattle trichomoniasis)","options":[{"name":"Tritrichomonas foetus-associated bovine trichomoniasis","supports":"Natural service, older carrier bulls, early reproductive loss and protozoa in preputial material.","against":"Requires validated test for T. foetus; organisms with similar morphology can be confused.","hist":"Often nonspecific endometritis, diagnosis supported by organism PCR/culture.","role":"best"},{"name":"Campylobacter fetus subsp. venerealis","supports":"Also causes venereal infertility and repeat breeding with apparently normal carrier bulls.","against":"Bacterial rather than protozoal organism; needs Campylobacter-specific testing.","hist":"Variable mild genital inflammation; histology is not specific.","role":"less"},{"name":"Bovine viral diarrhea virus","supports":"Can cause embryonic/fetal loss and poor reproductive performance.","against":"Bull preputial protozoa and venereal herd pattern favor trichomoniasis, not enough alone without testing.","hist":"Fetal infection may produce variable developmental or inflammatory lesions.","role":"less"},{"name":"Brucella abortus","supports":"Infectious infertility and abortion in cattle require consideration.","against":"More characteristically later abortion, necrotizing placentitis and fetal pneumonia rather than prominent early repeat-breeding pattern.","hist":"Placental necrosuppurative lesions and fetal pneumonia.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s71-hepatocutaneous","lecture":"71","system":"Skin","title":"Painful crusting footpads with a honeycomb liver","species":"Older diabetic dog","history":"An older dog with weight loss and poor body condition develops painful hyperkeratotic fissured footpads, crusts around mucocutaneous junctions, and recurrent superficial bacterial infection. Liver ultrasound reveals a distinctive honeycomb-like pattern.","gross":"Bilaterally symmetric fissured and crusting footpads plus periocular/perioral and pressure-point erosions; hepatic nodular regenerative/degenerative pattern.","histo":"Epidermal parakeratosis above vacuolated/edematous pale spinous cells and basal hyperplasia, giving the classic red-white-blue layering.","best":"Superficial necrolytic dermatitis (hepatocutaneous syndrome)","morph":"Skin: multifocal superficial necrolytic dermatitis; liver: associated metabolic/hepatocellular disorder","mechanism":"Severe metabolic derangements in amino acid availability and chronic hepatic dysfunction contribute to ineffective epidermal maintenance and superficial epidermal necrosis; this is a clinical histopathologic syndrome rather than a specific infectious agent.","tests":"Skin biopsies from suitable lesion edges, serum chemistry/amino acid assessment, hepatic imaging and workup for hepatic disease or rarer glucagonoma; culture secondary infection when needed.","ref":"Lecture 71 slide 39 — superficial necrolytic dermatitis","options":[{"name":"Superficial necrolytic dermatitis (hepatocutaneous syndrome)","supports":"Signalment, lesion distribution and microscopic pattern together provide the strongest match; agent or syndrome confirmation still requires the listed tests.","against":"Pattern recognition is suggestive rather than a stand-alone etiologic confirmation.","hist":"Epidermal parakeratosis above vacuolated/edematous pale spinous cells and basal hyperplasia, giving the classic red-white-blue layering.","role":"best"},{"name":"Zinc-responsive dermatosis","supports":"Footpad crusting and parakeratotic scale can overlap.","against":"Hepatic honeycomb pattern plus red-white-blue necrolytic histology favor hepatocutaneous syndrome.","hist":"Parakeratotic hyperkeratosis and epidermal hyperplasia without the diagnostic pale laminar epidermal necrolysis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Pemphigus foliaceus","supports":"Crusting pawpads and facial areas can occur.","against":"Subcorneal acantholytic pustules differ from broad laminar necrolytic epidermal change.","hist":"Subcorneal pustules with acantholytic keratinocytes.","role":"less"},{"name":"Canine hypothyroidism","supports":"Alopecia and recurrent bacterial dermatitis may occur.","against":"Painful fissured footpads and characteristic epidermal necrolysis are atypical.","hist":"Follicular atrophy/telogenization and variable hyperkeratosis, not red-white-blue epidermal layering.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s71-photosense","lecture":"71","system":"Skin","title":"White-skinned sheep with sun-exposed crusts and icterus","species":"Adult sheep","history":"A light-skinned sheep develops painful crusted erosions of ear tips, muzzle and unpigmented dorsal skin after turnout. Pigmented skin remains relatively spared; sclerae are icteric and the liver has cholestatic disease.","gross":"Ulcerative/crusted dermatitis of UV-exposed, sparsely haired unpigmented sites with evidence of hepatobiliary disease.","histo":"Epidermal necrosis, edema and variably neutrophilic interface/superficial inflammation; histology alone does not identify the photosensitizing compound.","best":"Hepatogenous (type III) photosensitization","morph":"Skin: photodistributed epidermal necrosis and ulceration associated with cholestasis","mechanism":"Chlorophyll metabolism produces phylloerythrin; impaired biliary clearance causes its accumulation in skin, where ultraviolet light generates reactive tissue injury.","tests":"Investigate hepatic/biliary injury, relevant plants/mycotoxins, liver enzymes and bilirubin; differentiate primary photosensitization by lack of hepatobiliary involvement.","ref":"Lecture 71 slides 25–26 — types I, II and III photosensitization","options":[{"name":"Hepatogenous (type III) photosensitization","supports":"Signalment, lesion distribution and microscopic pattern together provide the strongest match; agent or syndrome confirmation still requires the listed tests.","against":"Pattern recognition is suggestive rather than a stand-alone etiologic confirmation.","hist":"Epidermal necrosis, edema and variably neutrophilic interface/superficial inflammation; histology alone does not identify the photosensitizing compound.","role":"best"},{"name":"Primary type I photosensitization","supports":"Photodistributed unpigmented-skin injury looks similar.","against":"Usually direct ingestion/contact with photodynamic agent without required cholestasis.","hist":"Photo-induced epidermal necrosis; histology usually cannot identify the exposure class.","role":"less"},{"name":"Dermatophilosis","supports":"Crusts can form on the face and dorsum of sheep.","against":"Wet-weather bacterial crusts are not obligatorily limited to unpigmented UV-exposed skin or associated with icterus.","hist":"Neutrophilic exudative epidermitis containing branching segmented bacterial filaments.","role":"less"},{"name":"Cutaneous lupus erythematosus","supports":"Sun-exacerbated muzzle/ear lesions may occur.","against":"Underlying cholestasis and strict depigmented photodistribution favor photosensitization.","hist":"Lymphocytic interface dermatitis and basal keratinocyte injury.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s72-greasy-pig","lecture":"72","system":"Skin","title":"Piglets covered in dark greasy exudate","species":"2-week-old nursing piglets","history":"Several piglets in a litter develop erythema around face and ears followed by rapidly spreading sticky brown exudate and dehydration. The epidermis sloughs superficially and deeper subcutaneous abscesses are absent.","gross":"Diffuse superficial greasy brown crusts and exudate, initially facial/aural then generalizing.","histo":"Superficial/subcorneal acantholysis or pustules with crusting, bacterial cocci and limited deep dermal invasion; toxin-mediated epidermal exfoliation.","best":"Exudative epidermitis (greasy pig disease; toxigenic Staphylococcus hyicus)","morph":"Skin: diffuse superficial exfoliative and exudative bacterial epidermitis","mechanism":"Exfoliative toxins produced by certain S. hyicus strains disrupt keratinocyte adhesion, allowing marked superficial exudation; dehydration and electrolyte disturbance can become severe in small piglets.","tests":"Skin lesion culture and confirmation of relevant toxin-producing staphylococcal infection when needed; assess hydration and hygiene/predisposing skin injury.","ref":"Lecture 72 slide 38 — greasy pig disease","options":[{"name":"Exudative epidermitis (greasy pig disease; toxigenic Staphylococcus hyicus)","supports":"Signalment, lesion distribution and microscopic pattern together provide the strongest match; agent or syndrome confirmation still requires the listed tests.","against":"Pattern recognition is suggestive rather than a stand-alone etiologic confirmation.","hist":"Superficial/subcorneal acantholysis or pustules with crusting, bacterial cocci and limited deep dermal invasion; toxin-mediated epidermal exfoliation.","role":"best"},{"name":"Porcine juvenile pustular psoriasiform dermatitis","supports":"Young pigs can have crusting and scaling.","against":"Noninfectious psoriasiform lesions do not usually produce overwhelming sticky toxigenic exudate and dehydration.","hist":"Psoriasiform epidermal hyperplasia and pustulation rather than typical staphylococcal exfoliation.","role":"less"},{"name":"Swinepox","supports":"Piglets can develop generalized papules, pustules and crusts.","against":"Discrete pox papules and epidermal viral inclusions differ from spreading greasy exfoliative dermatitis.","hist":"Ballooning degeneration and intracytoplasmic poxviral inclusions.","role":"less"},{"name":"Porcine erysipelas","supports":"Can produce erythematous skin lesions and systemic illness.","against":"Diamond-shaped vasculitic infarcts rather than diffuse greasy superficial epidermal crusts.","hist":"Dermal vascular injury, thrombosis and necrosis.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s72-dermatophilus","lecture":"72","system":"Skin","title":"Rain-soaked horse with paintbrush crusts","species":"Adult horse","history":"After persistent rain, a horse develops tightly adherent crusts over the back and rump. Hair tufts lift with crusts, leaving a moist erythematous surface. Impression cytology from the underside of a fresh crust reveals branching rows of small coccoid forms.","gross":"Tufted or paintbrush-like exudative crusts in chronically wet hair-bearing skin.","histo":"Neutrophilic exudative epidermitis with segmented branching gram-positive organisms forming transverse and longitudinal rows (railroad-track appearance).","best":"Dermatophilosis (Dermatophilus congolensis)","morph":"Skin: multifocal exudative neutrophilic bacterial dermatitis with crusting","mechanism":"Moisture and epidermal microtrauma permit zoospores to infect superficial skin; cycles of bacterial multiplication and epidermal inflammation produce laminated crusts.","tests":"Fresh-crust impression cytology/Gram stain; culture or PCR where indicated. Avoid using unrelated fungal branching hyphae as substitutes for dermatophilic filament morphology.","ref":"Lecture 72 slides 36–37 — dermatophilosis","options":[{"name":"Dermatophilosis (Dermatophilus congolensis)","supports":"Signalment, lesion distribution and microscopic pattern together provide the strongest match; agent or syndrome confirmation still requires the listed tests.","against":"Pattern recognition is suggestive rather than a stand-alone etiologic confirmation.","hist":"Neutrophilic exudative epidermitis with segmented branching gram-positive organisms forming transverse and longitudinal rows (railroad-track appearance).","role":"best"},{"name":"Dermatophytosis","supports":"Alopecia, scale and broken hairs may resemble some cases.","against":"Fungal arthroconidia on hair shafts rather than segmented branching bacteria in exudative crust.","hist":"Fungal hyphae in keratin and hair follicles.","role":"less"},{"name":"Staphylococcal superficial pyoderma","supports":"Crusting and follicular pustules can occur.","against":"Cocci lack characteristic longitudinal/transverse branching filaments.","hist":"Follicular pustules with cocci and neutrophils.","role":"less"},{"name":"Cutaneous habronemiasis","supports":"Horse with summer skin erosions and crusting.","against":"Habronema lesions are typically exuberant pruritic granulomatous wounds at fly-exposed sites, not rain-associated paintbrush crusts.","hist":"Eosinophilic granulomatous dermatitis and parasite fragments.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s73-habronema","lecture":"73","system":"Skin","title":"Horse summer sore that will not heal","species":"Adult horse","history":"In summer a horse develops a persistently pruritic nonhealing ulcerated exuberant granulation lesion near the medial canthus and distal limb. Several hard yellow granules are found in the lesion. Biopsy reveals abundant eosinophils.","gross":"Ulcerated, proliferative, pruritic nodules or plaques with yellow gritty necrotic granules at fly-accessible sites.","histo":"Marked eosinophilic granulomatous dermatitis, collagen flame figures and occasional degenerating spirurid larvae within eosinophilic foci.","best":"Cutaneous habronemiasis (summer sores)","morph":"Skin: multifocal eosinophilic granulomatous dermatitis with ulceration","mechanism":"Flies carrying third-stage Habronema/Draschia larvae deposit them on wounds; larvae cannot complete their gastric life cycle in skin and provoke a strong hypersensitivity response.","tests":"Biopsy with serial levels and parasite search, epidemiologic context and exclusion of sarcoid or pythiosis; larvae are often absent from sections.","ref":"Lecture 73 slide 24 — summer sores","options":[{"name":"Cutaneous habronemiasis (summer sores)","supports":"Signalment, lesion distribution and microscopic pattern together provide the strongest match; agent or syndrome confirmation still requires the listed tests.","against":"Pattern recognition is suggestive rather than a stand-alone etiologic confirmation.","hist":"Marked eosinophilic granulomatous dermatitis, collagen flame figures and occasional degenerating spirurid larvae within eosinophilic foci.","role":"best"},{"name":"Pythiosis (Pythium insidiosum)","supports":"Equine proliferative ulcerating lesions can contain eosinophilic kunkers.","against":"Broad sparsely septate oomycete hyphae in kunkers and strongly progressive invasive granulomas favor pythiosis.","hist":"Eosinophilic granulomas with broad hyphae demonstrable by GMS/PAS or immunostaining.","role":"less"},{"name":"Equine sarcoid","supports":"Persistent nodular ulcerated lesion is possible.","against":"Eosinophilic granules and larvae-associated inflammatory pattern favor habronemiasis.","hist":"Fibroblastic spindle-cell dermal tumor with epidermal hyperplasia.","role":"less"},{"name":"Proud flesh / exuberant granulation tissue","supports":"Chronic equine wounds may form exuberant pink tissue.","against":"Pronounced eosinophilia and yellow gritty foci suggest a specific parasitic/oomycete process.","hist":"Capillary-rich fibrovascular granulation tissue rather than eosinophilic granulomas.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s73-pythium","lecture":"73","system":"Skin","title":"Wetland horse with destructive ulcer and kunkers","species":"Horse with access to warm standing water","history":"A horse develops a rapidly progressive ulcerative granulomatous distal limb lesion after frequent contact with stagnant water. Irregular hard yellow kunkers protrude from sinuses. Biopsy contains extensive eosinophilic necrosis with broad sparsely septate hyphae.","gross":"Severe proliferative ulcerated cutaneous mass, sinus tracts and gritty necrotic kunkers.","histo":"Pyogranulomatous and eosinophilic inflammation with broad sparsely septate oomycete hyphae surrounded by eosinophilic necrotic debris; immunohistochemistry/PCR aid identification.","best":"Cutaneous pythiosis (Pythium insidiosum)","morph":"Skin/subcutis: severe eosinophilic necrotizing pyogranulomatous dermatitis with oomycete hyphae","mechanism":"Aquatic Pythium zoospores encyst in damaged skin and invade tissue, triggering eosinophilic granulomatous inflammation and progressive tissue destruction.","tests":"Biopsy lesion margin/kunkers for histology, immunohistochemistry, PCR or culture by qualified lab. Pythium is an oomycete, not a true fungus.","ref":"Lecture 73 slide 31 — Pythium insidiosum","options":[{"name":"Cutaneous pythiosis (Pythium insidiosum)","supports":"Signalment, lesion distribution and microscopic pattern together provide the strongest match; agent or syndrome confirmation still requires the listed tests.","against":"Pattern recognition is suggestive rather than a stand-alone etiologic confirmation.","hist":"Pyogranulomatous and eosinophilic inflammation with broad sparsely septate oomycete hyphae surrounded by eosinophilic necrotic debris; immunohistochemistry/PCR aid identification.","role":"best"},{"name":"Cutaneous habronemiasis","supports":"Pruritic summer sores and yellow granules can closely resemble pythiosis.","against":"Parasite-associated lesion often less deeply invasive; finding spirurid larvae rather than hyphae confirms.","hist":"Eosinophilic granulomatous dermatitis with rare larval profiles.","role":"less"},{"name":"Basidiobolomycosis","supports":"Oomycete/fungal-like destructive eosinophilic granulomas may overlap in some regions.","against":"Different organisms and geographic context require identification.","hist":"Eosinophilic granulomas with fungal elements of different morphology.","role":"less"},{"name":"Equine sarcoid","supports":"Proliferative ulcerated dermal mass may mimic.","against":"Hyphae within destructive eosinophilic lesions exclude a purely fibrous neoplasm.","hist":"Dermal spindle-cell fibroblastic neoplasia without hyphae.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s74-pemphigus-vulgaris","lecture":"74","system":"Skin","title":"Dog with painful oral ulcers and skin blisters","species":"Adult dog","history":"A dog has painful erosions of the oral mucosa, mucocutaneous junctions and groin. Intact blisters are rare. Biopsy from an early lesion edge shows epidermal separation just above the basal keratinocyte layer.","gross":"Fragile bullae or erosions especially at mucosa/mucocutaneous surfaces, potentially generalized.","histo":"Suprabasal acantholysis with a row of basal keratinocytes remaining on basement membrane (tombstones); variable secondary inflammation.","best":"Pemphigus vulgaris","morph":"Skin and oral mucosa: suprabasal acantholytic vesiculobullous dermatitis","mechanism":"Autoantibodies to desmosomal proteins disrupt keratinocyte cohesion at the suprabasal epidermal level, producing deep blistering and erosion.","tests":"Biopsy intact edge of early vesicle (not an old ulcer) for histology and correlate with mucosal distribution; rule out infectious acantholytic mimics.","ref":"Lecture 74 slide 12 — pemphigus vulgaris","options":[{"name":"Pemphigus vulgaris","supports":"Signalment, lesion distribution and microscopic pattern together provide the strongest match; agent or syndrome confirmation still requires the listed tests.","against":"Pattern recognition is suggestive rather than a stand-alone etiologic confirmation.","hist":"Suprabasal acantholysis with a row of basal keratinocytes remaining on basement membrane (tombstones); variable secondary inflammation.","role":"best"},{"name":"Pemphigus foliaceus","supports":"Can cause facial crusts, pustules, acantholysis.","against":"Subcorneal rather than suprabasal splitting; oral mucosal ulceration much less characteristic.","hist":"Subcorneal neutrophilic pustules containing acantholytic keratinocytes.","role":"less"},{"name":"Bullous pemphigoid","supports":"Oral and skin blisters may be painful.","against":"Subepidermal (basement membrane zone) split rather than suprabasal tombstoning.","hist":"Subepidermal vesicles with eosinophils/neutrophils.","role":"less"},{"name":"Toxic epidermal necrolysis","supports":"Widespread mucosal erosion and skin slough may mimic severe pemphigus.","against":"TEN is extensive full-thickness epidermal necrosis, not primarily suprabasal acantholysis.","hist":"Confluent keratinocyte apoptosis and full-thickness epidermal necrosis.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s74-eosinophilic-plaque","lecture":"74","system":"Skin","title":"Cat with pruritic weeping abdominal plaques","species":"Adult cat","history":"A cat develops intensely pruritic raised red moist sharply demarcated plaques on the ventral abdomen and inner thighs. Cytology has abundant eosinophils. Flea exposure and allergy history are possible.","gross":"Raised erythematous alopecic eroded moist plaques in ventral abdominal/medial thigh region.","histo":"Eosinophilic superficial-to-deep dermatitis with epidermal hyperplasia, spongiosis, eosinophilic exocytosis and possible eosinophilic collagen degeneration.","best":"Feline eosinophilic plaque (eosinophilic granuloma complex reaction pattern)","morph":"Skin: eosinophilic hyperplastic and erosive dermatitis (eosinophilic plaque)","mechanism":"Hypersensitivity-driven eosinophil recruitment causes epidermal and dermal inflammation; fleas, food allergy or other allergens may contribute. The plaque describes a reaction pattern, not a definitive underlying allergen.","tests":"Skin cytology and biopsy if atypical, rigorous flea control, investigation of food/environmental hypersensitivity and infection; avoid claiming cytology alone identifies etiology.","ref":"Lecture 74 slides 33–36 — feline eosinophilic granuloma complex","options":[{"name":"Feline eosinophilic plaque (eosinophilic granuloma complex reaction pattern)","supports":"Signalment, lesion distribution and microscopic pattern together provide the strongest match; agent or syndrome confirmation still requires the listed tests.","against":"Pattern recognition is suggestive rather than a stand-alone etiologic confirmation.","hist":"Eosinophilic superficial-to-deep dermatitis with epidermal hyperplasia, spongiosis, eosinophilic exocytosis and possible eosinophilic collagen degeneration.","role":"best"},{"name":"Indolent ulcer","supports":"Eosinophilic lesion of feline mucocutaneous tissue is related.","against":"Typically upper-lip ulcer, often minimally pruritic, rather than ventral moist plaque.","hist":"Ulcerated eosinophilic dermatitis of upper lip with variable collagen change.","role":"less"},{"name":"Eosinophilic granuloma","supports":"Can be linear thigh lesion or raised lip/chin plaque.","against":"Linear raised firm granuloma rather than large erythematous oozing plaque.","hist":"Dermal eosinophilic granulomatous reaction with collagen flame figures.","role":"less"},{"name":"Superficial bacterial pyoderma","supports":"Red moist erosive plaques and pruritus overlap.","against":"Heavy eosinophils with typical feline plaque distribution favor hypersensitivity pattern; bacteria should be checked.","hist":"Neutrophilic folliculitis/pustules with bacteria rather than eosinophilic-dominant dermatitis.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s71-cushing","lecture":"71","system":"Skin","title":"Endocrine skin: alopecia with comedones","species":"9-year-old dog","history":"An older dog develops progressive symmetrical truncal alopecia, thin skin, comedones and abdominal enlargement with polyuria and polydipsia. There is no primary severe itch.","gross":"Bilaterally symmetrical truncal alopecia, thin skin, comedones, possible calcinosis cutis.","histo":"Follicular and epidermal atrophy with telogenization and sometimes dermal collagen mineralization.","best":"Canine hyperadrenocorticism","morph":"Skin: chronic endocrine atrophy and follicular arrest","mechanism":"Excess glucocorticoid suppresses fibroblasts, collagen synthesis and hair follicle cycling, with muscle wasting and redistribution of fat.","tests":"Endocrine testing guided by clinical likelihood, urinalysis/culture, chemistry and evaluation for infection or hypothyroidism.","ref":"Lecture 71 slide 31","options":[{"name":"Canine hyperadrenocorticism","supports":"Lesion distribution, signalment, clinical context and the stated microscopic pattern together make this the strongest diagnosis.","against":"The gross pattern alone does not confirm the etiology; correlate appropriate testing and sampling.","hist":"Follicular and epidermal atrophy with telogenization and sometimes dermal collagen mineralization.","role":"best"},{"name":"Hypothyroidism","supports":"Symmetrical alopecia and seborrhea can overlap.","against":"PU/PD, potbelly, very thin skin and calcinosis are more consistent with glucocorticoid excess.","hist":"Telogenization, follicular atrophy; generally without calcinosis cutis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Seasonal flank alopecia","supports":"Symmetrical well-demarcated truncal alopecia occurs.","against":"Usually seasonal flank localization and no systemic PU/PD or skin thinning.","hist":"Follicular dysplasia and melanin clumping.","role":"less"},{"name":"Demodicosis","supports":"Alopecia and comedones can appear with adult-onset disease.","against":"Mites and folliculitis on deep skin scrapings/histology identify infestation.","hist":"Follicular mites, perifolliculitis, rupture and pyogranulomas.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s72-sheep-pox","lecture":"72","system":"Skin","title":"Sheep with generalized papules and systemic illness","species":"Young lamb","history":"Following fever, lymph node enlargement and respiratory signs, multiple lambs develop firm raised papules and crusted nodules over hairless skin; necropsy also shows nodular pulmonary lesions.","gross":"Generalized firm papules and nodules with crusting in sparsely wooled sites; occasional lung and internal nodules.","histo":"Epidermal hyperplasia/ballooning and dermal inflammatory infiltrates, with characteristic poxviral cytoplasmic inclusions in suitable lesions.","best":"Sheeppox (capripoxvirus)","morph":"Skin: multifocal proliferative viral dermatitis with systemic lesions","mechanism":"Capripoxvirus causes systemic infection and epithelial/dermal injury; unlike localized orf, susceptible sheep may show severe generalized and visceral disease.","tests":"Official laboratory PCR for capripoxvirus and applicable reportable-disease response; avoid assuming lesion type identifies virus without testing.","ref":"Lecture 72 slides 6–7","options":[{"name":"Sheeppox (capripoxvirus)","supports":"Lesion distribution, signalment, clinical context and the stated microscopic pattern together make this the strongest diagnosis.","against":"The gross pattern alone does not confirm the etiology; correlate appropriate testing and sampling.","hist":"Epidermal hyperplasia/ballooning and dermal inflammatory infiltrates, with characteristic poxviral cytoplasmic inclusions in suitable lesions.","role":"best"},{"name":"Contagious ecthyma (orf)","supports":"Young sheep develop crusted poxviral lesions.","against":"Orf typically targets lips/muzzle/teats and is less likely to cause systemic pulmonary nodules.","hist":"Proliferative epidermitis with ballooning degeneration and pox inclusions.","role":"less"},{"name":"Dermatophilosis","supports":"Crusts may occur over skin.","against":"Typically exudative paintbrush crusts with branching segmented bacterial organisms, not systemic pox nodules.","hist":"Neutrophilic crusts containing railroad-track bacterial filaments.","role":"less"},{"name":"Lumpy skin disease","supports":"Capripoxvirus skin nodules may look similar.","against":"Predominantly cattle rather than sheep; must verify host and organism.","hist":"Deep dermal vasculitis and necrotizing nodules with possible pox inclusions.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s72-fowlpox","lecture":"72","system":"Skin","title":"Chicken with comb crusts and oral plaques","species":"Young flock chicken","history":"Several chickens have raised crusted lesions on combs and wattles; some others show adherent diphtheritic plaques in the oral cavity and difficult breathing.","gross":"Dry form: proliferative crusting featherless skin; wet form: necrotic diphtheritic oral/upper respiratory plaques.","histo":"Hyperplastic epithelium with ballooning keratinocytes and large eosinophilic intracytoplasmic Bollinger bodies.","best":"Fowlpox (avipoxvirus), mixed dry and wet forms","morph":"Skin/oral mucosa: proliferative poxviral dermatitis and diphtheritic stomatitis","mechanism":"Avipoxvirus replication in epithelium creates ballooning and inclusions; mucosal plaques can physically obstruct airflow.","tests":"Histopathology showing typical inclusion bodies, virus PCR when confirmation needed; assess flock mosquito exposure and vaccination history.","ref":"Lecture 72 slide 10","options":[{"name":"Fowlpox (avipoxvirus), mixed dry and wet forms","supports":"Lesion distribution, signalment, clinical context and the stated microscopic pattern together make this the strongest diagnosis.","against":"The gross pattern alone does not confirm the etiology; correlate appropriate testing and sampling.","hist":"Hyperplastic epithelium with ballooning keratinocytes and large eosinophilic intracytoplasmic Bollinger bodies.","role":"best"},{"name":"Infectious laryngotracheitis","supports":"Birds may develop dyspnea and upper airway exudate.","against":"ILT causes respiratory mucosal lesions with intranuclear herpesviral inclusions rather than characteristic cutaneous fowlpox.","hist":"Necrotizing tracheitis with intranuclear inclusions early.","role":"less"},{"name":"Vitamin A deficiency","supports":"Oral white plaques and keratinization may occur in birds.","against":"No typical poxvirus intracytoplasmic Bollinger bodies or comb scabs.","hist":"Squamous metaplasia of respiratory/oral epithelium.","role":"less"},{"name":"Bacterial dermatitis","supports":"Crusts and necrotic skin plaques could appear.","against":"Bacteria do not produce large viral Bollinger bodies.","hist":"Suppurative/necrotizing dermatitis with bacterial colonies.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s72-erysipelas","lecture":"72","system":"Skin","title":"Pig with geometric purple skin patches","species":"6-month-old grower pig","history":"A febrile pig is reluctant to rise and has multiple raised sharply demarcated rhomboid pink-purple skin plaques. Another herd mate has swollen joints.","gross":"Multifocal rhomboid or diamond-shaped erythematous to purple skin lesions, occasionally progressing to necrosis.","histo":"Small-vessel vasculitis, thrombosis and dermal ischemic necrosis; possible septic thrombi.","best":"Porcine erysipelas (Erysipelothrix rhusiopathiae)","morph":"Skin: multifocal dermal vascular infarction, consistent with septicemia","mechanism":"Erysipelothrix bacteremia and vascular injury impair dermal perfusion producing the diamond pattern; chronic forms may feature arthritis or vegetative endocarditis.","tests":"Culture/PCR from appropriate tissues/blood in consultation with laboratory, assess joints/heart and zoonotic exposure precautions.","ref":"Lecture 72 slide 39","options":[{"name":"Porcine erysipelas (Erysipelothrix rhusiopathiae)","supports":"Lesion distribution, signalment, clinical context and the stated microscopic pattern together make this the strongest diagnosis.","against":"The gross pattern alone does not confirm the etiology; correlate appropriate testing and sampling.","hist":"Small-vessel vasculitis, thrombosis and dermal ischemic necrosis; possible septic thrombi.","role":"best"},{"name":"Classical swine fever or African swine fever","supports":"Both can cause fever and hemorrhagic/purple skin.","against":"Diamond-shaped urticarial infarcts are suggestive of erysipelas but hemorrhagic systemic diseases need regulatory testing when plausible.","hist":"Severe systemic hemorrhage, lymphoid necrosis or vascular injury depending agent.","role":"less"},{"name":"Porcine dermatitis and nephropathy syndrome","supports":"Purple dermal lesions and systemic illness possible.","against":"Typically necrotizing vasculitis with renal lesions in older pigs; investigate renal changes and PCV2 context.","hist":"Systemic immune-complex vasculitis and glomerulonephritis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Greasy pig disease","supports":"Can affect young pigs with exudative skin lesions.","against":"Diffuse greasy brown crusts rather than sharply demarcated purple vascular infarcts.","hist":"Superficial exfoliative epidermitis with staphylococci.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s72-sarcoid","lecture":"72","system":"Skin","title":"Horse with recurring fibroblastic skin nodule","species":"6-year-old horse","history":"A persistent nodular mass over the girth area enlarges after previous trauma and regrowth following removal. Grossly the lesion is firm to fleshy, ulcerated in part, but there is no evidence of distant metastasis.","gross":"Nodular/fibroblastic proliferative cutaneous mass with variable verrucous surface or ulceration.","histo":"Dermal interlacing spindle-shaped fibroblastic cells with whorled bundles and epidermal hyperplasia; invasive local pattern without typical hematogenous metastasis.","best":"Equine sarcoid (BPV-associated dermal fibroblastic tumor)","morph":"Skin: locally invasive nonmetastasizing fibroblastic neoplasm","mechanism":"Bovine papillomavirus-associated transformation of dermal fibroblasts leads to locally invasive tumor growth and recurrence after incomplete removal.","tests":"Histopathology and clinical context; handle biopsy/treatment planning carefully because trauma may exacerbate some lesions.","ref":"Lecture 72 slide 16","options":[{"name":"Equine sarcoid (BPV-associated dermal fibroblastic tumor)","supports":"Lesion distribution, signalment, clinical context and the stated microscopic pattern together make this the strongest diagnosis.","against":"The gross pattern alone does not confirm the etiology; correlate appropriate testing and sampling.","hist":"Dermal interlacing spindle-shaped fibroblastic cells with whorled bundles and epidermal hyperplasia; invasive local pattern without typical hematogenous metastasis.","role":"best"},{"name":"Exuberant granulation tissue","supports":"Pink proliferative wound tissue can resemble fibroblastic sarcoid.","against":"Usually associated with injury and histologically abundant perpendicular capillaries and organized healing tissue.","hist":"Vascular granulation tissue with fibroblasts, no characteristic sarcoid architecture.","role":"less"},{"name":"Squamous cell carcinoma","supports":"Ulcerated locally invasive skin mass can occur.","against":"Neoplastic squamous epithelial islands and keratin pearls, not primarily dermal fibroblasts.","hist":"Invasive atypical squamous cell epithelium.","role":"less"},{"name":"Fibrosarcoma","supports":"Dermal spindle cell malignant tumor can resemble sarcoid.","against":"May show more marked cellular atypia and metastatic potential; histology and clinical setting distinguish.","hist":"Infiltrative malignant spindle-cell tumor with variable atypia.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s73-sarcoptes","lecture":"73","system":"Skin","title":"Itchy dog with crusted ear margins","species":"Young dog from crowded kennel","history":"Several dogs are severely pruritic with papules and crusts on pinnal margins, elbows and hocks; people handling them develop transient itchy papules. A superficial scrape may be negative.","gross":"Severe itchy papular and crusting dermatitis on ear edges, ventral body and pressure areas, with excoriations.","histo":"Superficial perivascular hypersensitivity dermatitis, epidermal hyperplasia and hyperkeratosis; burrowing mites and eggs may be seen in stratum corneum but are often missed.","best":"Sarcoptic mange (Sarcoptes scabiei)","morph":"Skin: crusting hypersensitivity dermatitis with surface/epidermal mites","mechanism":"Burrowing mites stimulate a strong hypersensitivity response; mite burden may be low despite intense itch and zoonotic transient papules.","tests":"Multiple superficial scrapings, compatible signs, contact history and response to appropriate veterinary treatment; consider flea allergy and atopy.","ref":"Lecture 73 slide 8","options":[{"name":"Sarcoptic mange (Sarcoptes scabiei)","supports":"Lesion distribution, signalment, clinical context and the stated microscopic pattern together make this the strongest diagnosis.","against":"The gross pattern alone does not confirm the etiology; correlate appropriate testing and sampling.","hist":"Superficial perivascular hypersensitivity dermatitis, epidermal hyperplasia and hyperkeratosis; burrowing mites and eggs may be seen in stratum corneum but are often missed.","role":"best"},{"name":"Canine generalized demodicosis","supports":"Mites and follicular dermatitis can cause alopecia and scaling.","against":"Demodex resides in hair follicles and often has folliculitis; scabies is highly contagious and intensely pruritic.","hist":"Follicular mites, folliculitis/furunculosis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Flea allergy dermatitis","supports":"Severe itch and papules common.","against":"More strongly lumbosacral/tail base and flea evidence; ear margins characteristic of scabies.","hist":"Perivascular eosinophilic dermatitis without burrowing mites.","role":"less"},{"name":"Canine atopic dermatitis","supports":"Chronic intensely pruritic dermatitis common.","against":"Onset and distribution plus contagious kennel cluster favor scabies; mites must be ruled out.","hist":"Spongiotic/perivascular hypersensitivity dermatitis, no mites.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s73-ringworm","lecture":"73","system":"Skin","title":"Shelter kitten with circular scaling alopecia","species":"4-month-old kitten","history":"A kitten develops expanding circular alopecic scaly plaques on the face and forelimbs. A foster caregiver has a round itchy patch; a sampled broken hair contains fungal arthroconidia.","gross":"Circular alopecia, scale, broken hairs and variable follicular papules, sometimes little itch.","histo":"Dermatophytic infection of hair shaft and follicular keratin with hyphae and arthroconidia; perifolliculitis may be present.","best":"Dermatophytosis (Microsporum canis)","morph":"Skin/hair: fungal folliculitis with ectothrix arthroconidial hair infection","mechanism":"Dermatophyte infects keratinized hair and stratum corneum; contaminated hairs/spores transmit infection to humans and animals.","tests":"Fungal culture, PCR interpreted with examination, trichography and Wood's lamp screening where appropriate; examine contacts and environment.","ref":"Lecture 73 slide 27","options":[{"name":"Dermatophytosis (Microsporum canis)","supports":"Lesion distribution, signalment, clinical context and the stated microscopic pattern together make this the strongest diagnosis.","against":"The gross pattern alone does not confirm the etiology; correlate appropriate testing and sampling.","hist":"Dermatophytic infection of hair shaft and follicular keratin with hyphae and arthroconidia; perifolliculitis may be present.","role":"best"},{"name":"Demodicosis","supports":"Juvenile patchy alopecia and follicular scale may overlap.","against":"Deep scrapings/trichograms reveal mites rather than ectothrix fungal arthroconidia.","hist":"Intra-follicular mites, folliculitis/furunculosis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Superficial bacterial folliculitis","supports":"Alopecic patches and epidermal collarettes possible.","against":"Cocci/neutrophils and follicular pustules without fungal hairs support pyoderma.","hist":"Neutrophilic folliculitis with bacterial cocci.","role":"less"},{"name":"Malassezia dermatitis","supports":"Greasy scaling and hair loss can occur.","against":"Yeast on surface cytology and often malodor/erythema differ from dermatophyte-infected hairs.","hist":"Surface budding yeast with variable superficial inflammation.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s73-malassezia","lecture":"73","system":"Skin","title":"Malodorous dog with greasy skin folds","species":"Adult allergic dog","history":"A dog with chronic pruritus develops greasy erythematous darkened interdigital and axillary skin with a musty odor. Tape cytology shows multiple peanut-shaped budding yeasts.","gross":"Erythematous greasy malodorous folds, lichenification/hyperpigmentation and variable alopecia.","histo":"Superficial perivascular dermatitis, acanthosis and hyperkeratosis; budding yeast in surface keratin; organism number and clinical context matter.","best":"Malassezia pachydermatis dermatitis","morph":"Skin: chronic superficial yeast-associated dermatitis with lichenification","mechanism":"Commensal yeast overproliferation or hypersensitivity occurs when skin barrier and local environment change, often secondary to atopy or endocrinopathy.","tests":"Tape strip or impression cytology plus underlying allergy/endocrine investigation; culture rarely necessary for straightforward cases.","ref":"Lecture 73 slide 28","options":[{"name":"Malassezia pachydermatis dermatitis","supports":"Lesion distribution, signalment, clinical context and the stated microscopic pattern together make this the strongest diagnosis.","against":"The gross pattern alone does not confirm the etiology; correlate appropriate testing and sampling.","hist":"Superficial perivascular dermatitis, acanthosis and hyperkeratosis; budding yeast in surface keratin; organism number and clinical context matter.","role":"best"},{"name":"Staphylococcal superficial pyoderma","supports":"Red pruritic skin with scale, collarettes or odor can overlap.","against":"Neutrophils with cocci and pustular folliculitis point to bacterial infection; coinfection is common.","hist":"Suppurative folliculitis containing cocci.","role":"less"},{"name":"Canine atopic dermatitis without yeast overgrowth","supports":"Interdigital and axillary pruritus common.","against":"Cytology demonstrating increased yeast in a compatible lesion supports yeast involvement, although allergy may be the root cause.","hist":"Spongiotic/perivascular dermatitis; no characteristic yeast abundance.","role":"less"},{"name":"Dermatophytosis","supports":"Scaling alopecia and fungal infection overlap.","against":"Dermatophyte invades hair shafts; yeast resides on skin surface.","hist":"Hyphae or arthroconidia within/in association with hair.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s74-dle","lecture":"74","system":"Skin","title":"Depigmenting nasal planum in a collie","species":"7-year-old collie","history":"A dog develops progressive loss of normal cobblestone nasal planum texture, depigmentation, erythema and superficial erosions exacerbated by sun exposure, without systemic illness.","gross":"Symmetric nasal depigmentation and smooth loss of cobblestone surface with crusts and scarring.","histo":"Lymphocytic interface dermatitis, basal keratinocyte apoptosis/vacuolation and pigmentary incontinence, sometimes thickened basement membrane.","best":"Canine discoid lupus erythematosus (cutaneous lupus)","morph":"Nasal planum: chronic lymphocytic interface dermatitis","mechanism":"Localized immune attack on basal epidermal keratinocytes promotes interface damage and pigment loss; UV light can exacerbate lesions.","tests":"Biopsy active depigmented/erythematous lesion edge for histology; exclude nasal pyoderma, dermatophytosis and systemic lupus signs.","ref":"Lecture 74 slide 19","options":[{"name":"Canine discoid lupus erythematosus (cutaneous lupus)","supports":"Lesion distribution, signalment, clinical context and the stated microscopic pattern together make this the strongest diagnosis.","against":"The gross pattern alone does not confirm the etiology; correlate appropriate testing and sampling.","hist":"Lymphocytic interface dermatitis, basal keratinocyte apoptosis/vacuolation and pigmentary incontinence, sometimes thickened basement membrane.","role":"best"},{"name":"Mucocutaneous pyoderma","supports":"Crusts and erosions at mucocutaneous junction can resemble DLE.","against":"Bacteria/neutrophils and response to therapy may distinguish; histology can overlap.","hist":"Neutrophilic interface and superficial bacterial inflammation.","role":"less"},{"name":"Vitiligo","supports":"Symmetrical nasal depigmentation possible.","against":"Usually depigmentation without erosions or interface ulceration.","hist":"Melanocyte loss with minimal interface inflammation.","role":"less"},{"name":"Pemphigus foliaceus","supports":"Facial crusting and depigmentation occur.","against":"Subcorneal pustules/acantholysis differ from lymphocytic interface dermatitis.","hist":"Subcorneal acantholytic pustules.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s73-demodex-cati","lecture":"73","system":"Skin","title":"Cat with follicular facial mites","species":"Adult immunosuppressed cat","history":"An adult cat with a history of systemic illness has patchy facial and periocular alopecia, follicular scaling and occasional comedones. Deep scrapings reveal elongated mites deep inside follicles.","gross":"Patchy scaling alopecia with follicular plugs on head and neck.","histo":"Numerous slender mites within hair follicles/sebaceous structures, with perifolliculitis and sometimes follicular rupture.","best":"Feline Demodex cati","morph":"Skin: follicular parasitic dermatitis with Demodex mites","mechanism":"D. cati is follicle-dwelling; overgrowth is associated with altered host defenses and differs from contagious surface-dwelling D. gatoi.","tests":"Deep skin scrapings/trichograms plus evaluation for predisposing illnesses; species identification by mite morphology and location.","ref":"Lecture 73 slide 6","options":[{"name":"Feline Demodex cati","supports":"The specific host, distribution, exposure and microscopic lesion pattern provide the best combined evidence.","against":"The morphology still requires appropriate confirmation and cannot establish all etiologic details independently.","hist":"Numerous slender mites within hair follicles/sebaceous structures, with perifolliculitis and sometimes follicular rupture.","role":"best"},{"name":"Demodex gatoi","supports":"Feline demodicosis and overgrooming can resemble it.","against":"Superficial and contagious with short-bodied mites; deep follicular mites fit D. cati.","hist":"Short surface mites within stratum corneum, not elongated follicular mites.","role":"less"},{"name":"Dermatophytosis","supports":"Facial alopecia, scale, contagious risk.","against":"Fungal infection of hair shafts rather than follicular mite bodies.","hist":"Hyphae and arthroconidia around/in affected hairs.","role":"less"},{"name":"Feline acne","supports":"Comedones and follicular plugging can occur.","against":"Usually chin-centered keratinization, not numerous follicular mites.","hist":"Follicular keratin plugs and secondary inflammation, no mites.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s73-chorioptes","lecture":"73","system":"Skin","title":"Draft horse stamping with pastern crusts","species":"Draft horse with feathered fetlocks","history":"Several horses in a barn stamp and rub their hind fetlocks. There is scaling, alopecia and crusting over pasterns and distal limbs; superficial scrapings reveal nonburrowing mites.","gross":"Distal limb papules, scale, crusts, chronic thickening in feathered pasterns.","histo":"Superficial eosinophilic/perivascular dermatitis with epidermal hyperplasia and mites on keratin surface rather than deep within follicles.","best":"Chorioptic mange (Chorioptes spp.)","morph":"Distal-limb parasitic crusting dermatitis","mechanism":"Chorioptes feed on skin debris and are surface dwellers, causing irritant/hypersensitivity responses at characteristic distal limb sites.","tests":"Superficial skin scrapings or tape from crust margins; differentiate from pastern dermatitis, dermatophilosis and mites of other genera.","ref":"Lecture 73 slide 12","options":[{"name":"Chorioptic mange (Chorioptes spp.)","supports":"The specific host, distribution, exposure and microscopic lesion pattern provide the best combined evidence.","against":"The morphology still requires appropriate confirmation and cannot establish all etiologic details independently.","hist":"Superficial eosinophilic/perivascular dermatitis with epidermal hyperplasia and mites on keratin surface rather than deep within follicles.","role":"best"},{"name":"Psoroptic mange","supports":"Nonburrowing mites, pruritus and crusts in horses.","against":"Psoroptes more often involve mane/ear canal depending species; morphology confirms genus.","hist":"Mites on skin/ear debris, superficial dermatitis; histology rarely genus-specific.","role":"less"},{"name":"Equine pastern dermatitis","supports":"Crusting and swelling over pasterns match.","against":"A multifactorial syndrome; demonstrable Chorioptes supports mite component.","hist":"Variable neutrophilic/eosinophilic dermatitis, with or without mites.","role":"less"},{"name":"Dermatophilosis","supports":"Wet crusting on lower legs can occur.","against":"Segmented branching bacteria are seen in crust cytology rather than mites.","hist":"Neutrophilic crusts with Dermatophilus filaments.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s73-psoroptes","lecture":"73","system":"Skin","title":"Rabbit shaking head with thick ear crusts","species":"Adult rabbit","history":"A rabbit develops marked head shaking and scratching, with thick tan-brown laminated crust filling the external auditory canal and pinna. Debris contains large nonburrowing mites.","gross":"Severe crusting otitis and adherent ear exudate with marked discomfort.","histo":"Hyperkeratosis and exudative superficial dermatitis with mites in ear debris; histology alone is not needed for definitive mite identification.","best":"Psoroptes cuniculi-associated ear mange","morph":"Ear canal: severe parasitic crusting otitis externa","mechanism":"Surface-feeding psoroptic mites induce intense irritation and exudation; mites remain at skin surface and form heavy crusts.","tests":"Microscopic examination of a safely obtained ear crust/debris sample, mite morphological confirmation, assessment of contacts.","ref":"Lecture 73 slide 11","options":[{"name":"Psoroptes cuniculi-associated ear mange","supports":"The specific host, distribution, exposure and microscopic lesion pattern provide the best combined evidence.","against":"The morphology still requires appropriate confirmation and cannot establish all etiologic details independently.","hist":"Hyperkeratosis and exudative superficial dermatitis with mites in ear debris; histology alone is not needed for definitive mite identification.","role":"best"},{"name":"Otodectes cynotis","supports":"Ear mites can cause head shaking and otitis.","against":"Different mite genus/host pattern; large psoroptic mites and thick crusts are classic for rabbit ear canker.","hist":"Surface mites and hyperkeratotic crust; genus determined on direct examination.","role":"less"},{"name":"Bacterial otitis externa","supports":"Crusting, pain and exudate occur.","against":"Mites visible within crust rather than bacteria alone.","hist":"Neutrophilic exudate/bacteria without characteristic mites.","role":"less"},{"name":"Chorioptic mange","supports":"Surface mite causing pruritus and scaling.","against":"Usually distal limbs or perineal body sites rather than heavy rabbit ear canker.","hist":"Surface mites with superficial hyperkeratosis; mite morphology needed.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s73-cheyletiella","lecture":"73","system":"Skin","title":"Walking dandruff in a multi-pet household","species":"Young rabbit","history":"A rabbit and another household pet develop dorsal white flaky scale and variable pruritus. Brushing collects large superficial mites with prominent palpal claws; a caregiver develops transient itchy papules.","gross":"Prominent dry dorsal midline scale, variable erythema and alopecia.","histo":"Surface-located mites with mild superficial perivascular dermatitis and hyperkeratosis; often not seen on routine biopsy.","best":"Cheyletiellosis (walking dandruff)","morph":"Skin: superficial parasitic scaling dermatitis","mechanism":"Cheyletiella mites live on the surface; their feeding creates scale and hypersensitivity and can cause temporary human skin lesions.","tests":"Coat brushing, acetate tape or superficial scrapings and microscopic mite identification; treat in-contact animals under veterinarian guidance.","ref":"Lecture 73 slide 13","options":[{"name":"Cheyletiellosis (walking dandruff)","supports":"The specific host, distribution, exposure and microscopic lesion pattern provide the best combined evidence.","against":"The morphology still requires appropriate confirmation and cannot establish all etiologic details independently.","hist":"Surface-located mites with mild superficial perivascular dermatitis and hyperkeratosis; often not seen on routine biopsy.","role":"best"},{"name":"Sarcoptic mange","supports":"Contagious pruritic mite infestation and human transient lesions overlap.","against":"Sarcoptes burrows in superficial epidermis, usually intense itch and ear/elbow lesions in dogs.","hist":"Burrowing mites/eggs in stratum corneum and hypersensitivity dermatitis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Psoroptic mange","supports":"Surface mite with scale and itch.","against":"Often heavy ear crust in rabbits and different mite morphology.","hist":"Superficial mites within exudative ear crust.","role":"less"},{"name":"Dermatophytosis","supports":"Zoonotic scaling/alopecia can occur.","against":"Fungal infected hairs, not walking surface mites.","hist":"Arthroconidia/hyphae involving hair shafts.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s73-sporothrix","lecture":"73","system":"Skin","title":"Cat with ulcerated nodules tracking up limb","species":"Adult outdoor cat","history":"An outdoor cat has multiple draining ulcers and subcutaneous nodules on a forelimb with new nodules ascending proximally along lymphatic drainage. The handler reports a scratch and a new skin lesion.","gross":"Multiple ulcerating nodules and draining tracts, sometimes along lymphatics; cats may have extensive organism-rich wounds.","histo":"Pyogranulomatous dermatitis with round-to-oval/cigar-shaped yeast forms; organisms may be numerous in feline lesions.","best":"Feline sporotrichosis (Sporothrix spp.)","morph":"Skin/subcutis: nodular ulcerating pyogranulomatous fungal dermatitis","mechanism":"Sporothrix inoculated through trauma causes local and lymphocutaneous infection; infected cats can transmit viable organisms by scratches/bites and secretions.","tests":"Cytology/histopathology plus fungal culture or PCR with appropriate laboratory biosafety and zoonotic precautions; distinguish from atypical mycobacterial infection.","ref":"Lecture 73 slide 30","options":[{"name":"Feline sporotrichosis (Sporothrix spp.)","supports":"The specific host, distribution, exposure and microscopic lesion pattern provide the best combined evidence.","against":"The morphology still requires appropriate confirmation and cannot establish all etiologic details independently.","hist":"Pyogranulomatous dermatitis with round-to-oval/cigar-shaped yeast forms; organisms may be numerous in feline lesions.","role":"best"},{"name":"Atypical mycobacterial panniculitis","supports":"Nodules, draining tracts and pyogranulomatous dermatitis possible.","against":"Acid-fast bacilli rather than budding yeasts; special stains and PCR needed.","hist":"Pyogranulomatous panniculitis with acid-fast bacilli in some cases.","role":"less"},{"name":"Phaeohyphomycosis","supports":"Subcutaneous fungal nodules and ulceration may overlap.","against":"Dematiaceous pigmented hyphae and yeast-like bodies, not typical Sporothrix morphology.","hist":"Brown pigmented fungal cells/hyphae, highlighted by special stains.","role":"less"},{"name":"Cutaneous cryptococcosis","supports":"Cats can have ulcerative fungal nodules, often facial.","against":"Encapsulated narrow-based budding yeasts with mucicarmine-positive capsule.","hist":"Granulomatous inflammation with large encapsulated yeasts.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s73-zinc","lecture":"73","system":"Skin","title":"Husky with crusting around eyes and muzzle","species":"2-year-old Siberian husky","history":"A young husky eating a commercially complete diet develops symmetric adherent scale and crust around eyelids, lips, pinnae and elbows without major systemic abnormalities.","gross":"Symmetrical parakeratotic crusting at periorificial and pressure sites.","histo":"Marked parakeratotic hyperkeratosis with epidermal hyperplasia; inflammation varies and is not pathognomonic.","best":"Zinc-responsive dermatosis type I","morph":"Skin: parakeratotic hyperkeratotic dermatitis","mechanism":"In susceptible northern breeds decreased zinc absorption/utilization can impair epithelial cornification despite adequate dietary zinc; histology supports but does not uniquely prove etiology.","tests":"Dietary review and serum zinc interpretation with caution, skin biopsy, exclusion of other causes; veterinary-supervised zinc supplementation response supports diagnosis.","ref":"Lecture 73 slides 34–35","options":[{"name":"Zinc-responsive dermatosis type I","supports":"The specific host, distribution, exposure and microscopic lesion pattern provide the best combined evidence.","against":"The morphology still requires appropriate confirmation and cannot establish all etiologic details independently.","hist":"Marked parakeratotic hyperkeratosis with epidermal hyperplasia; inflammation varies and is not pathognomonic.","role":"best"},{"name":"Zinc-responsive dermatosis type II","supports":"Similar symmetric parakeratotic crusts.","against":"More often rapidly growing large-breed pups with deficient or antagonistic diets, not an adult northern breed on adequate diet.","hist":"Parakeratosis and epidermal hyperplasia similar; history decisive.","role":"less"},{"name":"Superficial necrolytic dermatitis","supports":"Periorificial and footpad crusts overlap.","against":"Usually older dogs with liver disease, painful pads and red-white-blue epidermal change.","hist":"Parakeratosis with pale upper spinous epidermis and basal proliferation.","role":"less"},{"name":"Pemphigus foliaceus","supports":"Crusts on muzzle and pinnae common.","against":"Acantholytic subcorneal pustules differ from primarily parakeratotic hyperkeratosis.","hist":"Neutrophilic subcorneal pustules and acantholytic cells.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s74-pemphigoid","lecture":"74","system":"Skin","title":"Dog with tense blisters and mucosal erosions","species":"Adult collie","history":"A dog has painful relatively tense blisters on skin and oral mucosa, with erosions after rupture. A fresh-edge biopsy shows the entire epidermis lifted cleanly from dermis.","gross":"Vesicles/bullae and erosions on skin and mucosa, sometimes extensive.","histo":"Subepidermal separation at the basement membrane zone with eosinophils and neutrophils and an intact overlying epidermis.","best":"Bullous pemphigoid","morph":"Skin: subepidermal vesiculobullous autoimmune dermatitis","mechanism":"Autoantibodies targeting hemidesmosomal/basement membrane zone components disrupt dermal-epidermal attachment, unlike desmosomal disruption in pemphigus.","tests":"Biopsy intact bullae or fresh edge for H&E; immunofluorescence/serologic testing where available; avoid ulcer center with lost junction.","ref":"Lecture 74 slides 14–15","options":[{"name":"Bullous pemphigoid","supports":"The specific host, distribution, exposure and microscopic lesion pattern provide the best combined evidence.","against":"The morphology still requires appropriate confirmation and cannot establish all etiologic details independently.","hist":"Subepidermal separation at the basement membrane zone with eosinophils and neutrophils and an intact overlying epidermis.","role":"best"},{"name":"Pemphigus vulgaris","supports":"Oral erosions and fragile bullae overlap.","against":"Split is suprabasal intraepidermal with tombstone basal cells.","hist":"Suprabasal acantholysis retaining basal keratinocytes.","role":"less"},{"name":"Pemphigus foliaceus","supports":"Facial crusting, fragile pustules possible.","against":"Split is very superficial and intraepidermal rather than junctional.","hist":"Subcorneal acantholytic pustules.","role":"less"},{"name":"Epidermolysis bullosa","supports":"Junctional separation and bullae can resemble it.","against":"Usually inherited and begins young, rather than acquired adult immune disease.","hist":"Blister level depends on inherited structural protein defect.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s74-flea","lecture":"74","system":"Skin","title":"Dog chewing tail base with papular crusts","species":"Adult dog without reliable flea prevention","history":"The dog bites the rump and tail base, developing papules, crusts and traumatic alopecia over the lumbosacral region. Only one flea is found despite severe itch.","gross":"Lumbosacral and tail-base excoriations, erythematous papules, crusts and secondary alopecia.","histo":"Superficial perivascular eosinophilic hypersensitivity dermatitis; lesions are not specific for flea saliva.","best":"Flea allergy dermatitis","morph":"Skin: pruritic eosinophilic hypersensitivity dermatitis, flea-associated pattern","mechanism":"Hypersensitivity to flea saliva can cause profound inflammation from minimal flea exposure, so a low observed flea count does not rule out flea allergy.","tests":"Flea combing, environmental and all-pet flea control trial, exclusion of secondary pyoderma and other allergic disease.","ref":"Lecture 74 slide 32","options":[{"name":"Flea allergy dermatitis","supports":"The specific host, distribution, exposure and microscopic lesion pattern provide the best combined evidence.","against":"The morphology still requires appropriate confirmation and cannot establish all etiologic details independently.","hist":"Superficial perivascular eosinophilic hypersensitivity dermatitis; lesions are not specific for flea saliva.","role":"best"},{"name":"Canine atopic dermatitis","supports":"Pruritus and recurrent erythematous skin can overlap.","against":"Atopy commonly involves paws, ears, flexures, face; tail-base concentration and fleas support FAD.","hist":"Nonspecific perivascular allergic dermatitis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Sarcoptic mange","supports":"Severe itch and crusts, may have few mites.","against":"Ear margins, elbows, hocks, contagion and human exposure favor Sarcoptes.","hist":"Superficial burrowing mites with hypersensitivity if detected.","role":"less"},{"name":"Malassezia dermatitis","supports":"Can produce intense itch, greasy odor and erythema.","against":"Yeast surface cytology plus fold distribution and smell favor yeast over primary flea reaction.","hist":"Surface budding yeast with chronic superficial dermatitis.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s74-culicoides","lecture":"74","system":"Skin","title":"Horse rubbing mane and tail every summer","species":"Adult horse living near wetlands","history":"Each warm season the horse rubs its mane and tail until hair breaks and the skin becomes lichenified and crusted. Lesions lessen in cold months and cluster near midges.","gross":"Excoriation, alopecia, crusting and lichenification along mane and tail head with summer recurrence.","histo":"Superficial and deep perivascular eosinophilic dermatitis, epidermal hyperplasia; histology is a hypersensitivity pattern, not proof of insect type.","best":"Equine Culicoides hypersensitivity (sweet itch)","morph":"Skin: chronic pruritic eosinophilic hypersensitivity dermatitis","mechanism":"IgE-mediated and other hypersensitivity to salivary antigens from biting Culicoides midges leads to seasonal self-trauma and chronic secondary changes.","tests":"Seasonality, lesion pattern and midge exposure plus exclusion of ectoparasites; avoid treating biopsy alone as Culicoides-specific.","ref":"Lecture 74 slide 31","options":[{"name":"Equine Culicoides hypersensitivity (sweet itch)","supports":"The specific host, distribution, exposure and microscopic lesion pattern provide the best combined evidence.","against":"The morphology still requires appropriate confirmation and cannot establish all etiologic details independently.","hist":"Superficial and deep perivascular eosinophilic dermatitis, epidermal hyperplasia; histology is a hypersensitivity pattern, not proof of insect type.","role":"best"},{"name":"Chorioptic mange","supports":"Pruritic crusts and rubbing possible.","against":"Commonly distal limbs and feathered pasterns, with surface mites identified.","hist":"Superficial parasitic dermatitis with mites.","role":"less"},{"name":"Onchocerca dermatitis","supports":"Equine vector-associated itch and pigment changes possible.","against":"Onchocerca microfilariae may be detected in skin and distribution can involve ventral midline/eyes.","hist":"Eosinophilic dermatitis sometimes with filarial microfilariae.","role":"less"},{"name":"Atopic dermatitis","supports":"Seasonal allergy and pruritus also possible.","against":"Mane/tail distribution and midge contact suggest insect-specific hypersensitivity but do not prove it.","hist":"Perivascular eosinophilic dermatitis indistinguishable on H&E.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s74-em","lecture":"74","system":"Skin","title":"Dog with target lesions and keratinocyte apoptosis","species":"Adult dog after recent medication change","history":"A dog develops acute symmetric erythematous annular and target-like skin lesions, with limited mucocutaneous erosion. Early biopsy demonstrates scattered necrotic keratinocytes and lymphocytes surrounding individual dying cells.","gross":"Erythematous annular target-like plaques, variable erosions; severity and mucosal involvement vary.","histo":"Lymphocytic interface dermatitis with single-cell keratinocyte apoptosis/satellitosis rather than full-thickness sheet necrosis.","best":"Erythema multiforme","morph":"Skin: acute apoptotic lymphocytic interface dermatitis","mechanism":"A dysregulated cell-mediated cytotoxic response against keratinocytes follows possible drug, infectious or neoplastic triggers; EM is distinct in the lecture from severe SJS/TEN patterns.","tests":"Biopsy fresh active lesions and careful drug/infection history; evaluate extent of detachment, mucosal injury and systemic instability.","ref":"Lecture 74 slide 20","options":[{"name":"Erythema multiforme","supports":"The specific host, distribution, exposure and microscopic lesion pattern provide the best combined evidence.","against":"The morphology still requires appropriate confirmation and cannot establish all etiologic details independently.","hist":"Lymphocytic interface dermatitis with single-cell keratinocyte apoptosis/satellitosis rather than full-thickness sheet necrosis.","role":"best"},{"name":"Toxic epidermal necrolysis","supports":"Severe acute epidermal injury and mucosal erosions can occur.","against":"TEN features widespread confluent full-thickness epidermal necrosis and detachment; do not equate targetoid EM with TEN.","hist":"Sheetlike epidermal necrosis with sparse inflammation.","role":"less"},{"name":"Discoid lupus erythematosus","supports":"Interface keratinocyte damage and facial erosions overlap.","against":"Chronic nasal-specific UV-associated lesions and basement membrane/pigment changes differ from widespread acute targets.","hist":"Chronic lymphocytic interface damage and pigmentary incontinence.","role":"less"},{"name":"Pemphigus vulgaris","supports":"Mucosal erosions and skin blisters overlap.","against":"Primarily suprabasal acantholysis rather than scattered apoptotic keratinocytes.","hist":"Suprabasal acantholytic intraepidermal clefts.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s74-mosquito","lecture":"74","system":"Skin","title":"Cat with seasonal pinna and nasal bridge ulcers","species":"Young outdoor cat","history":"During mosquito season, a cat develops recurrent crusted erosions on ear tips, dorsal nose and periocular skin. Cytology and biopsy show many eosinophils without organisms.","gross":"Symmetric papules, erosions and crusts of sparsely haired facial skin and ears.","histo":"Superficial/deep perivascular to interstitial eosinophilic dermatitis and epidermal erosion, sometimes eosinophilic collagen degeneration.","best":"Feline mosquito-bite hypersensitivity","morph":"Skin: eosinophilic allergic ulcerative dermatitis of pinnae and nasal bridge","mechanism":"Local hypersensitivity to mosquito salivary antigens drives eosinophil infiltration in exposed hairless skin.","tests":"Seasonal history and environmental insect exposure, elimination of dermatophytosis, mites, autoimmune/infectious disease and response to preventing bites.","ref":"Lecture 74 slide 40","options":[{"name":"Feline mosquito-bite hypersensitivity","supports":"The specific host, distribution, exposure and microscopic lesion pattern provide the best combined evidence.","against":"The morphology still requires appropriate confirmation and cannot establish all etiologic details independently.","hist":"Superficial/deep perivascular to interstitial eosinophilic dermatitis and epidermal erosion, sometimes eosinophilic collagen degeneration.","role":"best"},{"name":"Pemphigus foliaceus","supports":"Facial/pinna crusts and pustules can be similar.","against":"Subcorneal acantholytic pustules rather than dominant eosinophilic dermal infiltrates.","hist":"Acantholytic subcorneal pustular dermatitis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Notoedric mange","supports":"Cats can have intensely itchy facial and ear-edge crusts.","against":"Burrowing mites on scrapings and contact spread favor Notoedres.","hist":"Mites/eggs in cornified layer with crusting hypersensitivity.","role":"less"},{"name":"Feline eosinophilic granuloma complex","supports":"Allergic eosinophilic lesions may overlap histologically.","against":"Distribution and bite seasonality, not histology alone, implicate mosquitoes.","hist":"Eosinophilic granulomatous or plaque-form dermatitis depending syndrome.","role":"less"}]}]};
